    </node>
    <node id="National Market Surveillance Authorities">
      <data key="d0">National Market Surveillance Authorities</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">National Market Surveillance Authorities are bodies within member states responsible for overseeing regulated credit institutions and ensuring compliance with financial regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;National market surveillance authorities are responsible for the oversight of AI systems at a national level, ensuring compliance and safety.&lt;SEP&gt;National market surveillance authorities are the entities within each Member State responsible for enforcing compliance with the Union harmonisation legislation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8da189e7622be67a80b2e12945a657cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f12c01099dadb1fc1008ce18c7d800e8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b90abf0636c26d546e9b6724e3224ae</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_161.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_74.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="General-Purpose AI Models">
      <data key="d0">General-Purpose AI Models</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">General-Purpose AI Models are versatile artificial intelligence systems designed to perform a wide range of tasks without being specifically tailored to any single application. Their broad adaptability and functionality allow them to be applied across various sectors and domains, making them significant advancements within the AI landscape. These models serve as foundational technologies for numerous downstream systems and applications, highlighting their critical role in the evolution of AI.

As General-Purpose AI Models are implemented widely, they come under specific regulatory obligations and compliance measures. Competent authorities oversee their use to ensure adherence to established safety and ethical standards, which are vital for responsible deployment. Evaluations are conducted to assess potential systemic risks associated with these models, particularly when classified as high-risk AI systems. 

In addition to their adaptability, these models have the capability to generate various types of content, including text and images, which creates both opportunities and challenges for creators across multiple fields. Many General-Purpose AI Models are made available under free and open-source licenses, promoting transparency and accessibility in the AI community while balancing innovation with the necessary oversight to mitigate related risks. Overall, General-Purpose AI Models signify a notable evolution in AI technology, combining versatility with a commitment to safety and responsible use.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c49e78c25e30ed3a2557915010f14f3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-212b902a4529db42951674775ac3ec73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-40a9e2a0fc4601f4b3d4f583384b6a06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-47ce3f90343791af10ffae2a7848f26e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-af991f85166d6ddfbbf84e4a73207d3d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98532c431ec75ae38c76dc0730337a16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-649ac2acd7b1a3b0b6a81ce20aa799f7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-769497b509433ed0e4746ff50bbdec69&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-32556db3a80f72f1db354810bba21be7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae3ab8a3b5c5d0c61e8b99fa718913b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-11bdaa862215570a86eed137e3092ea6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-577494efd5ce842563e6974b0da9707b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b90abf0636c26d546e9b6724e3224ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6570d0db42cf764ebb073d69da0137e3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5429fed5961ecea1e5eed049e922476f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bee8a141261944c2193bcb00b304b768&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cfbca03bf6afb99c3326dc8b02d083b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-161f3120961644fbdc17c7b09b46e54b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b3f46abe104a1bdb0fae1c25094db36d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69b5f24963810305f2a7846d54923bf0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8c259012a2ff719418b0deb6cbd52130</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_108.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_97.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_104.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_111.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_105.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_110.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_53.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_133.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_162.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_101.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_116.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_161.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_169.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_114.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_179.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_102.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_111.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_55.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_66.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747657453</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Compliance Evaluations">
      <data key="d0">Compliance Evaluations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Compliance evaluations are processes conducted by market surveillance authorities to assess whether AI systems meet the established regulatory requirements.&lt;SEP&gt;Compliance evaluations assess whether AI systems and models adhere to legal and regulatory requirements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f4ac0ccf9bd954306cee6efbfd342416&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0b90abf0636c26d546e9b6724e3224ae</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_161.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_75.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cross-Border Cases">
      <data key="d0">Cross-Border Cases</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cross-border cases involve regulatory issues that span multiple jurisdictions, requiring cooperation between different national authorities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0b90abf0636c26d546e9b6724e3224ae</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_161.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="mutatis mutandis">
      <data key="d0">mutatis mutandis</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Mutatis mutandis is a Latin phrase used in legal and philosophical contexts meaning 'with the necessary changes having been made' or 'the necessary adjustments being made'. It indicates that something remains valid despite changes in circumstances.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4f5323e3b01efa8f64e095d14892512c</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_161.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="European Harmonised Standards">
      <data key="d0">European Harmonised Standards</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">European Harmonised Standards are regulations that provide a framework for compliance for manufacturers and providers within the EU.&lt;SEP&gt;European Harmonised Standards refer to approved protocols that grant presumed conformity for compliance with obligations placed on providers of AI models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c49e78c25e30ed3a2557915010f14f3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cfbca03bf6afb99c3326dc8b02d083b</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_55.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_53.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Providers of General-Purpose AI Models">
      <data key="d0">Providers of General-Purpose AI Models</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Providers are organizations or entities that develop, market, or use general-purpose AI models and are obligated to ensure safety and compliance.&lt;SEP&gt;Providers of general-purpose AI models are entities responsible for developing, deploying, and maintaining AI systems that serve multiple applications.&lt;SEP&gt;Providers of general-purpose AI models are organizations responsible for creating and managing AI systems that can perform a wide range of tasks and pose systemic risks.&lt;SEP&gt;Providers of General-Purpose AI Models are organizations or entities that develop and supply AI models intended for widespread use in various applications.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-47ce3f90343791af10ffae2a7848f26e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bee8a141261944c2193bcb00b304b768&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cfbca03bf6afb99c3326dc8b02d083b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18d33fd192f06f727ac6bb21becbaa57</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_111.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_89.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_114.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_55.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747464861</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Articles 53 and 54">
      <data key="d0">Articles 53 and 54</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Articles 53 and 54 refer to specific sections of a regulatory framework that outline obligations for providers of general-purpose AI models with systemic risks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7cfbca03bf6afb99c3326dc8b02d083b</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_55.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Article 56">
      <data key="d0">Article 56</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Article 56 pertains to codes of practice that providers can use to demonstrate compliance with obligations until a harmonised standard is published.&lt;SEP&gt;Article 56 refers to a specific regulation within the legal framework governing AI models, which includes codes of practice for compliance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c49e78c25e30ed3a2557915010f14f3c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cfbca03bf6afb99c3326dc8b02d083b</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_55.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_53.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Systemic Risk">
      <data key="d0">Systemic Risk</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Systemic Risk relates to the potential negative impacts of AI models when they are integrated into broader systems, warranting regulatory obligations.&lt;SEP&gt;Systemic risk refers to the potential for widespread impact on the market and society resulting from the deployment of general-purpose AI models.&lt;SEP&gt;Systemic risk refers to the potential for widespread failure in the financial system, potentially highlighted in evaluations involving AI models.&lt;SEP&gt;Systemic risk refers to the potential for an event or failure in one part of a system, such as AI models, to cause widespread failure or negative impacts on the broader system or society.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b89491b02af0a00cbc13f9e8df827a6d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a0bef2c1dcc3d7b428c7611732f7ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-212b902a4529db42951674775ac3ec73&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cfbca03bf6afb99c3326dc8b02d083b</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_55.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_93.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_104.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_97.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747464756</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Model Evaluation">
      <data key="d0">Model Evaluation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Model Evaluation is the assessment of the model's performance based on various metrics to determine its effectiveness and reliability.&lt;SEP&gt;Model evaluation involves assessing the AI model's performance and risks using standardized protocols and tools.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7963b30391bfa3d5e28ecd9a95b61010&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cfbca03bf6afb99c3326dc8b02d083b</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_55.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_88.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Adversarial Testing">
      <data key="d0">Adversarial Testing</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Adversarial testing involves evaluating AI models to identify vulnerabilities and ensure they are resilient against malicious inputs.&lt;SEP&gt;Adversarial testing is a method used to identify vulnerabilities in AI models by challenging them with difficult or unexpected inputs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bee8a141261944c2193bcb00b304b768&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7cfbca03bf6afb99c3326dc8b02d083b</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_55.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_114.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="European Harmonised Standard">
      <data key="d0">European Harmonised Standard</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A European harmonised standard refers to standards that have been established to ensure compliance with specific regulations and directives within the European Union.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fce1cda52753ec9e5183762f5b2c81b1</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_55.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Approved Code of Practice">
      <data key="d0">Approved Code of Practice</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">An approved code of practice is a set of guidelines or standards that organizations are required to adhere to in order to ensure compliance with regulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fce1cda52753ec9e5183762f5b2c81b1</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_55.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Trade Secrets">
      <data key="d0">Trade Secrets</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Trade Secrets involve practices, designs, formulas, processes, or any type of information that provides a business advantage over competitors.&lt;SEP&gt;Trade secrets refer to confidential business information that provides a competitive edge to a company and is protected under confidentiality obligations.&lt;SEP&gt;Trade secrets encompass confidential business information that is protected under the Directive (EU) 2016/943, preventing unlawful acquisition, use, and disclosure.&lt;SEP&gt;Trade secrets are a form of intellectual property that comprises information that is not generally known or reasonably ascertainable by others, which gives a business a competitive edge.&lt;SEP&gt;Trade secrets are confidential practices, formulas, processes, or various types of information that provide a business with a competitive edge and must be legally protected.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-13b35a74627bd926656242d6545e09a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe4d7c8b1c65cb7b9f5021681b13c271&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7963b30391bfa3d5e28ecd9a95b61010&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9317b056c2a260423c79e368903cde7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fce1cda52753ec9e5183762f5b2c81b1</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_167.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_52.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_55.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_88.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_78.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747657442</data>
    </node>
    <node id="European Data Protection Board">
      <data key="d0">European Data Protection Board</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The European Data Protection Board advises on the regulation concerning data protection and privacy in the European Union.&lt;SEP&gt;The European Data Protection Board is responsible for ensuring consistent application of data protection rules across the EU.&lt;SEP&gt;The European Data Protection Board is tasked with ensuring that data protection rules are applied consistently across the European Union.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d1b92a727425c41637e16da3cb9ac9c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-472b03a027e3fcae21e86e23849e4510&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ae8bdbb57af24d78fe2313201571bea7</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_180.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_3.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Regulation (EU) 2018/1725">
      <data key="d0">Regulation (EU) 2018/1725</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Regulation (EU) 2018/1725 establishes rules for the processing of personal data by European Union institutions and bodies.&lt;SEP&gt;Regulation (EU) 2018/1725 is a comprehensive legal framework designed to protect personal data within the context of EU institutions and bodies, including the European Commission. It governs the processing of personal data by these entities, ensuring compliance with data protection laws and safeguarding the privacy of individuals. The regulation specifically addresses the rights of individuals concerning how their personal data is processed, promoting transparency and accountability in the management of personal information.

In addition to establishing rules for the secure and lawful handling of personal data, Regulation (EU) 2018/1725 facilitates the free movement of such data across the Union. It also provides guidelines for processing personal data in sandbox experimentation environments, ensuring that these processes conform to established data protection standards.

Moreover, this regulation interacts with AI regulations, highlighting its relevance in the evolving landscape of technology and data processing. Overall, Regulation (EU) 2018/1725 is critical in protecting individuals' rights regarding their personal data and serves as a foundational element in the EU's commitment to privacy and data protection.&lt;SEP&gt;Regulation (EU) 2018/1725 pertains to the protection of individuals with regard to the processing of personal data by the Union institutions and bodies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc038db2f25d511bf84cccc9550e10c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8833999c9ef9aa31a007f2e77ffea02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-15eee417af0ebe299ed66be76a0b845a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a46250ea72037444e96653239f2893b2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3105f8ed20ed5608a4571fe7b1d8f942&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-602b68fe088482c0037845fc0b520c30&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ac98f64f8751ad51246ca16d843f030&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-800752b66b6fe0cd70bb0290efc2fbbe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-166a35f517674f4ceba2754c65f88c5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-472b03a027e3fcae21e86e23849e4510&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-85605aee8aaac2bf4ad29d80d902148f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-498834b2d84eaf9afe4e5fb0df15933c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77093970e26d1be04734ed16ea88f777&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2806e288e0b71013209c4ad85ecaa38b</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_2.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_10.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_53.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_14.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_180.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_59.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_131.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_3.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_70.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_39.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_140.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747657601</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Article 42(1) and (2)">
      <data key="d0">Article 42(1) and (2)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Article 42(1) and (2) of Regulation (EU) 2018/1725 outlines the consultation process involving the European Data Protection Supervisor and the European Data Protection Board.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-472b03a027e3fcae21e86e23849e4510</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_180.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="18 June 2021">
      <data key="d0">18 June 2021</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">18 June 2021 is the date when the European Data Protection Supervisor and the European Data Protection Board delivered their joint opinion.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-472b03a027e3fcae21e86e23849e4510</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_180.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Article 79(1)">
      <data key="d0">Article 79(1)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Article 79(1) is a provision that defines the criteria under which a high-risk AI system is considered to present a risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Article 79(1) pertains to regulations governing the risks presented by high-risk AI systems and the requirements for traceability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aff0d16c0510c298943e3daa75fd581b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a21678b36bef336bf18e091bdfdb68f0</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_24.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_12.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747657382</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Article 72">
      <data key="d0">Article 72</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Article 72 focuses on post-market monitoring of high-risk AI systems to ensure their safe operation after deployment.&lt;SEP&gt;Article 72 refers to a legislative framework that discusses the requirements for post-market monitoring of high-risk AI systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4ca7c773df7242c37f506b8ad3db475b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aff0d16c0510c298943e3daa75fd581b</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_12.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_9.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Article 26(5)">
      <data key="d0">Article 26(5)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Article 26(5) outlines the monitoring requirements for the operation of high-risk AI systems during their lifecycle.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aff0d16c0510c298943e3daa75fd581b</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_12.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Annex III">
      <data key="d0">Annex III</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Annex III is a crucial segment of regulations governing high-risk artificial intelligence (AI) systems under European Union law. It details the categories and criteria for identifying high-risk AI systems along with the specific compliance measures required for their testing and operation. This section serves as a comprehensive framework that facilitates the classification of AI systems according to their risk levels, which is essential for ensuring their responsible deployment and regulatory scrutiny.

The provisions of Annex III include requirements for transparency and traceability in the operations of high-risk AI systems, emphasizing the need for robust logging capabilities to assist in compliance and evaluation processes. It mandates that systems classified as high-risk must be registered before they can enter the market or be deployed as services, ensuring adherence to high safety and ethical standards.

Furthermore, Annex III outlines the conditions necessary for AI system certifications, which have a validity period of four years and include provisions for re-assessment. It not only catalogues high-risk AI applications but also delineates the relevant assessment obligations that accompany them, as well as the procedures and requirements for demonstrating compliance.

Additionally, Annex III addresses specific legal implications for high-risk AI in areas such as law enforcement, migration, asylum, and border control management. Overall, it represents a vital regulatory framework that defines the appropriate classification of high-risk AI systems, the standards for assessing their risks and impacts, and the operational conditions essential for maintaining regulatory compliance and ethical integrity in the use of AI technologies.&lt;SEP&gt;Annex III outlines specific high-risk AI systems mentioned in the Regulation, detailing their characteristics and usage requirements.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ff61ec794793cf787d4a0473487a06f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8cb1edc11e8b8d0e952b15617a80f379&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb0291da832d9defa94aaf2262ad3761&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe49d95d50900e82a706b2766339562f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aff0d16c0510c298943e3daa75fd581b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6b4348ad8873ef2041aca94abc9297ee&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fdf8a675497acfbeb420b95621b7cb31&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2c1d05b847c03fe702172f787883fc33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-675b49fdde07175ec9a1afb693d1d877&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a0779dfb4a5c0f82f05a67985fe0224a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aa9e2916955e882c73231ed69851f6ec&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c72b31b17ef0accd3df4ab25a063163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-71f538bc77dea56a19150de3c7514990&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f116a95954c59b63ce3149e6283ed8e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a69b68c9998277692ac6c6341fcdcac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2118453abd880b63eddb91fa3806c3f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-397d6e2474c4f45e883f265653f2e715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77a0a99b47c288a96d7474c796efb766&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b71cdb20a8fa5befb7c30c90163cd416&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d64a2a65aec35f8304efaaee30e0034d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc8a3a685818e1a12850434d4ddcb5cb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de5a0c7f560be7dcbe4301aed20bcb98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69838403e8b59bb122436a0d25a9fc1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-99c78ffcf815fa82f05dcb7dc5871e1d</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_60.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_27.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_12.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_74.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_44.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_72.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_26.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_6.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_43.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_86.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_14.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_7.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_49.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_112.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_73.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_78.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747657985</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Event Logging">
      <data key="d0">Event Logging</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Event logging refers to the capability of high-risk AI systems to automatically record events over their operational lifetime for traceability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aff0d16c0510c298943e3daa75fd581b</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_12.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Traceability">
      <data key="d0">Traceability</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Traceability is the property that enables tracking the workflow and decision-making process in high-risk AI systems, facilitating accountability.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aff0d16c0510c298943e3daa75fd581b</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_12.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deployer">
      <data key="d0">Deployer</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Deployer is an entity that cooperates with the Provider in organizing the real-world testing of AI systems, ensuring informed participation.&lt;SEP&gt;The Deployer is defined as a natural or legal person or public authority responsible for implementing and utilizing high-risk AI systems under their authority, with the exclusion of personal non-professional use. This entity plays a crucial role in the deployment process, as it holds legal responsibilities and is tasked with ensuring the safe and effective operation of these systems. The Deployer must possess a thorough understanding of the AI system’s outputs to utilize them appropriately, which is essential for making informed decisions that can significantly affect individuals.

Moreover, the Deployer is required to adhere to risk mitigation guidelines provided by the Provider, ensuring compliance with regulatory measures. This involves adequate training and information dissemination to handle the associated risks effectively. By overseeing the proper use of high-risk AI systems, the Deployer ensures that these technologies are operated within a framework that prioritizes safety and accountability, thus playing an integral role in the responsible deployment of AI. Overall, the Deployer encompasses any individual or organization that operates an AI system under their authority, reinforcing the need for awareness regarding the risks and necessary mitigation measures.&lt;SEP&gt;The deployer is the individual or entity responsible for assessing and managing the risks associated with the use of AI systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee04f6104a24650416810ede28f3790b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2118453abd880b63eddb91fa3806c3f3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f6e14583352886a741ac1d51eada03a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4c4d127e5313d415dd94f6b6761da956&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-727225adfedcad573a12c2ed92e8ee1f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65f69156748df83d9fa179140a3fe2aa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7b5e606b14946a1b388f008868e8e155&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6a8bd64d122f5a09e9e3859bf77c6931&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b28a4b325cc05a6cc4897f58ccdf00f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e7d62750f1113d24ac155fcd2b2ef02c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4ca7c773df7242c37f506b8ad3db475b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2f186d6b95e69680f63fabb65ad924a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-71f538bc77dea56a19150de3c7514990</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_13.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_96.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_60.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_9.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_84.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_65.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_3.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_86.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_171.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_14.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_13.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747657577</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Public Authority">
      <data key="d0">Public Authority</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A public authority is an organization that operates under governmental authority and may deploy AI systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Public authorities are government organizations responsible for safeguarding public interests, including health and safety, within the context of the AI Regulatory Sandbox.&lt;SEP&gt;Public Authority refers to agencies or bodies that deploy AI systems and are responsible for ensuring compliance with AI regulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2b28a4b325cc05a6cc4897f58ccdf00f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72498ec90c19419f10373dde7e7b51b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-699b82035d71a633a86a67090e5a61e8</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_59.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_71.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_13.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747464910</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Agency">
      <data key="d0">Agency</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An agency is a body that can act on behalf of another organization and may deploy AI systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2b28a4b325cc05a6cc4897f58ccdf00f</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_13.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Legal Person">
      <data key="d0">Legal Person</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A legal person refers to any organization that possesses legal rights and obligations, capable of deploying AI systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Legal persons refer to organizations or entities, such as corporations and governments, that have legal rights and responsibilities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bb040a8b4e6e3fa6f26776f560b6591c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b28a4b325cc05a6cc4897f58ccdf00f</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_13.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_170.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Natural Person">
      <data key="d0">Natural Person</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A natural person is an individual human being, particularly in the context of being assessed for risks related to criminal offenses.&lt;SEP&gt;A natural person refers to an individual human being who can also deploy AI systems under certain conditions.&lt;SEP&gt;Natural persons are individual human beings who possess legal rights and obligations under the law.&lt;SEP&gt;Natural person defines any human individual participating in the testing of AI systems in real-world conditions, with certain rights and responsibilities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0c9405f93ec3e0ccf6209ed15ef0263f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b28a4b325cc05a6cc4897f58ccdf00f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-77ba8e0dd4981bd71dedb04a86b45601&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb040a8b4e6e3fa6f26776f560b6591c</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_3.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_59.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_170.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_13.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747657218</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Authority">
      <data key="d0">Authority</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Authority refers to a person or group having the relevant legal or official power to grant access or make decisions regarding physical spaces.&lt;SEP&gt;Authority refers to the power or right given to deployers to utilize AI systems within a defined scope.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4013941d0b165f5643b927164f2ffd1c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2b28a4b325cc05a6cc4897f58ccdf00f</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_19.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_13.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747464751</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Scientific Panel of Independent Experts">
      <data key="d0">Scientific Panel of Independent Experts</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The scientific panel consists of independent experts in the field of AI, tasked with providing expertise and objective assessments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6579442a2f027e643cf907006333003a</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_151.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AI Models">
      <data key="d0">AI Models</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">AI Models refer to general-purpose artificial intelligence systems that are designed to perform tasks typically associated with human intelligence. These systems can vary in capabilities and applications, encompassing both general-purpose and specialized models. AI Models are governed by specific regulations and are subject to compliance with established codes of practice and standards, ensuring their safe and effective implementation. Additionally, they are composed of algorithms that generate data outputs, including text and images, and are frequently utilized in the automation of content creation. Overall, AI Models highlight the importance of adherence to regulatory obligations for compliance and monitoring, promoting responsible use in various applications of artificial intelligence.&lt;SEP&gt;AI Models refer to specific implementations or designs of AI systems that are subject to regulation under Union Law, especially for ethics and safety purposes.&lt;SEP&gt;AI Models refer to systems that leverage artificial intelligence technologies, specifically general-purpose models that can be utilized across various applications and industries.&lt;SEP&gt;AI models encompass the algorithms and methodologies employed in AI systems, which must comply with specific legal standards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-90efb8f063f236753e4794005afa2a0d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4dc0271c68856282748506ff58069b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6579442a2f027e643cf907006333003a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-18d33fd192f06f727ac6bb21becbaa57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-daa2a9a43bd7a2482ad876b406b9e9c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3bb5eb0651d16c25633339b4b2cb2e46&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3105f8ed20ed5608a4571fe7b1d8f942&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fcd3af92cad1d85ebf561f059acb114&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9cac929d0048fe34377179e8ecde4a5c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-778cb9e68fcb25e3f810e3cea927f7b2</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_133.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_2.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_89.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_151.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_89.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_118.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_164.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_56.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_111.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747657250</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Independent Experts">
      <data key="d0">Independent Experts</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Independent Experts are professionals selected based on their expertise in AI, tasked with providing impartial contributions to the regulation enforcement efforts.&lt;SEP&gt;Independent experts are individuals or groups that the AI Office can involve to provide unbiased evaluations of compliance with regulations regarding AI models.&lt;SEP&gt;Independent Experts are professionals with specialized knowledge who can offer their expertise during the creation of AI codes of practice.&lt;SEP&gt;Independent experts are individuals appointed by the Commission to carry out evaluations on behalf of the Commission.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-23ab4f338bfa06297d4c996d7d54dc70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6579442a2f027e643cf907006333003a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4fcd3af92cad1d85ebf561f059acb114&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4dc0271c68856282748506ff58069b0</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_92.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_56.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_151.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_164.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Monitoring Activities">
      <data key="d0">Monitoring Activities</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Monitoring Activities involve the processes undertaken by the AI Office and the independent experts to ensure compliance with AI regulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6579442a2f027e643cf907006333003a</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_151.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AI Systems Regulation">
      <data key="d0">AI Systems Regulation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">AI Systems Regulation is a comprehensive legal framework designed to govern the assessment and compliance of artificial intelligence technologies across various sectors. It outlines specific cases where AI systems do not pose significant risk of harm, clarifying their intended use and limitations. This set of compliance guidelines establishes transparency obligations for AI systems under both Union and Member State law, ensuring that users and providers understand the functional requirements and responsibilities associated with high-risk AI systems before their introduction to the market.

Furthermore, the AI Systems Regulation specifies rules for the providers and deployers of AI systems within the Union, detailing requirements for transparency and usability, particularly for high-risk applications. It also emphasizes the need for real-time operation of AI systems, highlighting the importance of immediate functionality without significant delays. Overall, the regulation aims to foster safe and responsible AI deployment while upholding user rights and societal standards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4a65ec6b0fbd17afe47587686523a725&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-13991bdab077b3114e8913f89a1371e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0044fdddd0a21caecd46a12639588d98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c5fb48082255c480535475ddb098366a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-712b56cbfc73c46eacde9204fa4e5c49&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d1a48b7c6d78e001878383cb0acaf58</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_2.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_53.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_143.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_137.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_72.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_17.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747658040</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Member State Law">
      <data key="d0">Member State Law</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Member State Law consists of national legislations that may impose additional transparency obligations on deployers of AI systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Member State Law refers to the individual laws enacted by countries that are part of the European Union, which must align with EU directives.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-737358b61293029e1a6d7326160654fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0044fdddd0a21caecd46a12639588d98</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_94.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_137.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747657652</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transparency Obligations">
      <data key="d0">Transparency Obligations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Transparency Obligations refer to the requirements established for the disclosure of information related to the use and output of AI systems, ensuring accountability in their deployment.&lt;SEP&gt;Transparency Obligations refer to the requirements for informing individuals about interactions with AI systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-eb43ec5a01dc502046ab6ffbe3a25504&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0044fdddd0a21caecd46a12639588d98</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_132.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_137.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747464929</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Deployment of AI Systems">
      <data key="d0">Deployment of AI Systems</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Deployment of AI Systems involves the real-world application and utilization of AI technologies, which must adhere to various legal and regulatory frameworks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0044fdddd0a21caecd46a12639588d98</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_137.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Council">
      <data key="d0">Council</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Council is an organization composed of representatives from Member States, responsible for various legislative and administrative tasks related to the EU.&lt;SEP&gt;The Council, formally known as the Council of the European Union, is a vital institution within the European Union's institutional framework. It represents the governments of the EU member states and plays a key role in the legislative decision-making process. The Council works collaboratively with the European Parliament and the Commission to evaluate, amend, and review legislative proposals, including those concerning regulations on artificial intelligence systems. As a key decision-making body, the Council contributes to shaping EU legislation by considering reports and proposals that impact member states and their governance.&lt;SEP&gt;The Council refers to the Council of the European Union, which, along with the Parliament, plays a key role in the legislative process.&lt;SEP&gt;The Council refers to the Council of the European Union, where member state governments are represented, playing a vital role in decision-making.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b837dd750c95d041180fa3d735328672&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-de5a0c7f560be7dcbe4301aed20bcb98&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6001fd4ce94d732804de396c3240c65f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-762d138929f27b0266d948c0aad50daa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d9317b056c2a260423c79e368903cde7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8b474f9815c7033ca1ad8daff4012eff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eab6e21707d57be17832ef8dfd7180a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da5fbf959baa6ee8e86278864a9144cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a69b68c9998277692ac6c6341fcdcac</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_9.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_97.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_112.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_173.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_78.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747657131</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Delegated Acts">
      <data key="d0">Delegated Acts</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Delegated Acts refer to legal acts that the European Commission can adopt to supplement or amend non-essential elements of EU legislation.&lt;SEP&gt;Delegated acts are legislative measures that allow the Commission to supplement or amend existing legislative acts.&lt;SEP&gt;Delegated acts are measures adopted by the Commission to modify existing legislation, which includes the power to amend provisions in relation to AI systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Delegated acts refer to measures taken by the Commission to modify existing legislation relating to AI systems and their risk factors.&lt;SEP&gt;Delegated acts are legal acts that allow the European Commission to supplement or amend non-essential parts of legislative acts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fe49d95d50900e82a706b2766339562f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6001fd4ce94d732804de396c3240c65f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da5fbf959baa6ee8e86278864a9144cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7805654ce0fe0d8d9b6ba008562d2aa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-cb0291da832d9defa94aaf2262ad3761</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_108.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_97.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_7.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_173.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747464292</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Interinstitutional Agreement of 13 April 2016 on Better Law-Making">
      <data key="d0">Interinstitutional Agreement of 13 April 2016 on Better Law-Making</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Interinstitutional Agreement of 13 April 2016 on Better Law-Making outlines processes for improving the quality of EU legislation.&lt;SEP&gt;This agreement establishes principles for the drafting and adoption of better laws within the European Union.&lt;SEP&gt;This agreement outlines the principles for better legislative practices and is referenced as a guideline for the Commission's consultations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da5fbf959baa6ee8e86278864a9144cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-762d138929f27b0266d948c0aad50daa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b7fd511ed97ef1a87c9783c3425b7d4</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_97.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_173.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Article 6">
      <data key="d0">Article 6</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Article 6 is a section of the EU regulation that outlines the classification of AI systems into high-risk and non-high-risk categories.&lt;SEP&gt;Article 6 refers to specific provisions within EU legislation that detail the process and authority for adopting delegated acts.&lt;SEP&gt;Article 6 outlines the classification criteria for AI systems, determining whether they fall under the high-risk category based on their intended purpose.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-72498ec90c19419f10373dde7e7b51b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da5fbf959baa6ee8e86278864a9144cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4329be6d4972c392a3a71593d402998</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_97.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_25.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_71.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747657107</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Article 7">
      <data key="d0">Article 7</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Article 7 outlines additional provisions regarding the delegation of acts by the Commission and the roles of other institutions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da5fbf959baa6ee8e86278864a9144cc</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_97.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Article 11">
      <data key="d0">Article 11</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Article 11 describes the process for adopting and implementing delegated acts concerning various legislative measures.&lt;SEP&gt;Article 11 refers to a specific regulation detailing how technical documentation must be prepared for high-risk AI systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Article 11 of Regulation (EU) 2019/2144 pertains to implementing acts for artificial intelligence systems, emphasizing the safety components required.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da5fbf959baa6ee8e86278864a9144cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b9e2499910b26136c66e6e7a013d8f0b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fc356102e8bbeed5152b8bb0cda2ff6</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_97.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_109.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_23.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Article 43">
      <data key="d0">Article 43</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Article 43 details the procedures for conformity assessment that must be adhered to for CE marking.&lt;SEP&gt;Article 43 discusses compliance and procedural conditions related to the delegation of powers to the Commission.&lt;SEP&gt;Article 43 is a regulatory provision that outlines the conformity assessment procedures required for high-risk AI systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Article 43 refers to a specific provision in EU regulations that addresses the conditions under which high-risk AI systems may be authorized for use.&lt;SEP&gt;Article 43 outlines the conformity assessment procedures for high-risk AI systems as stipulated in the regulation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d1d974005cd87fc21678c732472ee11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e07ee6b3452d764ccd9733dceadb1ac7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9fc356102e8bbeed5152b8bb0cda2ff6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-29241e99bddf5af77eca441d2d8dbc1b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da5fbf959baa6ee8e86278864a9144cc</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_48.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_46.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_23.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_97.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_34.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747657631</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Article 47">
      <data key="d0">Article 47</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Article 47 details limitations and revocation processes concerning delegated acts by the Commission.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da5fbf959baa6ee8e86278864a9144cc</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_97.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Article 51">
      <data key="d0">Article 51</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Article 51 elaborates on the duration and conditions under which delegated powers may be extended or revoked.&lt;SEP&gt;Article 51 outlines specific conditions that, if met by an AI model, may trigger a qualified alert regarding its risks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da5fbf959baa6ee8e86278864a9144cc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-140dff8b8263721e25ea86a4104c273d</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_97.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_90.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Article 52">
      <data key="d0">Article 52</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Article 52 outlines further rules and considerations regarding the monitoring of delegated acts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-da5fbf959baa6ee8e86278864a9144cc</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_97.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Article 53">
      <data key="d0">Article 53</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Article 53 could refer to a specific regulation within a legal framework governing general-purpose AI models.&lt;SEP&gt;Article 53 outlines specific obligations for providers of general-purpose AI models, aiming to ensure safety and compliance.&lt;SEP&gt;Article 53 provides the formal procedural conditions for the adoption and enforcement of delegated acts.&lt;SEP&gt;Article 53 references certain provisions in the regulatory text related to the classification of AI systems, providing context for the risk assessment procedures.&lt;SEP&gt;Article 53 pertains to specified obligations that providers must follow under the regulatory framework set by the Commission.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7c553a0db4b39033e7fcfeddeb912aac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-daa2a9a43bd7a2482ad876b406b9e9c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d64a2a65aec35f8304efaaee30e0034d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1a0bef2c1dcc3d7b428c7611732f7ffc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-da5fbf959baa6ee8e86278864a9144cc</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_91.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_6.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_97.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_56.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_93.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747658139</data>
    </node>
    <node id="European Commission">
      <data key="d0">European Commission</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The European Commission is the executive arm of the European Union responsible for proposing legislation and implementing decisions.&lt;SEP&gt;The European Commission is the executive body of the European Union (EU), playing a crucial role in the governance and operational management of the EU. It is primarily responsible for proposing legislation, enforcing laws, and implementing a wide range of policies, including those related to biometric systems and artificial intelligence (AI). In its capacity, the Commission oversees compliance with EU laws and regulations, specifically concerning high-risk AI systems, and is actively involved in the regulatory processes that govern authorizations for such systems.

Additionally, the European Commission manages the development of cybersecurity policies and ensures the establishment of common data spaces. It is tasked with upholding treaties and managing the day-to-day affairs of the EU, which includes promoting legislative initiatives and ensuring their effective implementation by member states. Through these comprehensive responsibilities, the European Commission contributes significantly to the smooth functioning of the EU’s day-to-day operations and its broader legislative framework, particularly in the field of AI regulation.&lt;SEP&gt;The European Commission is the executive branch of the European Union responsible for implementing decisions and managing day-to-day operations, including assessing harmonised standards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e07ee6b3452d764ccd9733dceadb1ac7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-762d138929f27b0266d948c0aad50daa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2187eb3476528ac7c75b571eb3844f0e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7a69b68c9998277692ac6c6341fcdcac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f56b40e98f8949a37c3ab283b01f7050&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8eae051373e28d701c4893438950556b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0695d12d62d4fd15a528842231db1850&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-72498ec90c19419f10373dde7e7b51b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d08b537f9a5bb2b4f4bbdf442ba39bdd&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d64a2a65aec35f8304efaaee30e0034d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ec0d2d3cbe2a53a3bbf6373c775edf2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-712b56cbfc73c46eacde9204fa4e5c49&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-34333cde7ca701413cb4d0a0ef1129e1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f41337092de45470ab838aff17309ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c8a6b92fbe8d93eb878957c5ed60aa0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-166a35f517674f4ceba2754c65f88c5f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eab6e21707d57be17832ef8dfd7180a9&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-671e76af4a78aebc96c988f0504bfa99</data>
