    </node>
    <node id="Biometric Categorisation">
      <data key="d0">Biometric Categorisation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Biometric categorisation involves assigning individuals to categories based on biometric data such as sex, age, and physical characteristics.&lt;SEP&gt;Biometric categorization involves the use of biological data to identify individuals, particularly for law enforcement purposes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d1b92a727425c41637e16da3cb9ac9c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac04e8bc9dd9ff3998ce3c08f3800f57</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_16.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_3.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Online Marketplaces">
      <data key="d0">Online Marketplaces</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Online marketplaces are platforms where products are sold, allowing consumers to preview how products look on themselves using filters.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ac04e8bc9dd9ff3998ce3c08f3800f57</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_16.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Online Social Network Services">
      <data key="d0">Online Social Network Services</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Online social network services are platforms focused on content sharing, where filters can be applied for modifying pictures or videos.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ac04e8bc9dd9ff3998ce3c08f3800f57</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_16.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biometric Data">
      <data key="d0">Biometric Data</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Biometric Data encompasses a range of unique physical and behavioral characteristics utilized for the identification and classification of individuals. This includes well-known traits such as fingerprints and facial recognition data, as well as other attributes like facial expressions, voice patterns, age, hair color, eye color, and personality traits. These identifiers are frequently processed by artificial intelligence systems, particularly in law enforcement contexts, to ensure accurate identification and categorization of individuals. The handling of Biometric Data is subject to legal regulations, which are designed to safeguard privacy and security, as it constitutes a type of personal data derived from physiological or behavioral characteristics. Thus, Biometric Data plays a crucial role in modern identification and verification processes, while also being regulated to protect individuals' privacy.&lt;SEP&gt;Biometric data refers to personal data that is derived from biometric identification methods, often used within AI systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Biometric data refers to personal data that can be used to identify individuals through unique physical or behavioral characteristics.&lt;SEP&gt;Biometric Data refers to unique physical characteristics, such as fingerprints or facial recognition, that can be used for identification purposes in law enforcement.&lt;SEP&gt;Biometric data refers to unique identifiers such as facial recognition or fingerprints that can be captured and analyzed by both real-time and post systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1be166c61f466e23c3d8c143f6efd3bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-737358b61293029e1a6d7326160654fa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc0db03f57b007cfc458657e88e273ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2bccefc944ad0794c75bc69b977c88a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ac04e8bc9dd9ff3998ce3c08f3800f57&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b65e9562dd645622f1390ba1863fee19&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-62c8c9618d935cf8910927a7d518c1f2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee1f186f5aed070bc8e6e813caed9967&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3ac98f64f8751ad51246ca16d843f030&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3070277451614bdc67954ec8735dd80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2d1a48b7c6d78e001878383cb0acaf58&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bc038db2f25d511bf84cccc9550e10c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-eb43ec5a01dc502046ab6ffbe3a25504&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-498834b2d84eaf9afe4e5fb0df15933c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-61cbf514bcfe4869e269c00a2dc9e712</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_132.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_35.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_15.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_18.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_94.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_14.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_5.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_50.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_3.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_54.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_38.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_39.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_16.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_17.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_30.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747658040</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Commercial Service">
      <data key="d0">Commercial Service</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Commercial service refers to a service provided for profit, where biometric categorisation systems may be utilized as ancillary features.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ac04e8bc9dd9ff3998ce3c08f3800f57</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_16.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Filters">
      <data key="d0">Filters</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Filters are tools or functionalities applicable on online platforms that categorize or modify facial or body features for a specific purpose.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ac04e8bc9dd9ff3998ce3c08f3800f57</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_16.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Obligations Under the Regulation">
      <data key="d0">Obligations Under the Regulation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Obligations under the regulation refer to the compliance requirements that AI systems must meet, as mandated by the AI Office and related regulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f4ac0ccf9bd954306cee6efbfd342416</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_75.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Principle of Subsidiarity">
      <data key="d0">Principle of Subsidiarity</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The principle of subsidiarity dictates that decisions should be made at the most immediate level possible, which underpins the rationale for the Union to act.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0455405f9e173535e78c4ea589745cf4</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_176.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Article 5 TEU">
      <data key="d0">Article 5 TEU</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Article 5 of the Treaty on European Union outlines the principles of subsidiarity and proportionality guiding EU legislation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0455405f9e173535e78c4ea589745cf4</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_176.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Competent Authority">
      <data key="d0">Competent Authority</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Competent authority refers to the designated body or entity responsible for overseeing and enforcing regulations, including those relating to AI.&lt;SEP&gt;The competent authority is a regulatory body responsible for overseeing and ensuring compliance of high-risk AI systems with established requirements.&lt;SEP&gt;The competent authority refers to regulatory bodies in the EU responsible for ensuring compliance with legislation concerning high-risk AI systems and other regulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa2b7ae4447e7d43e6030d6793c59ef7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe76292b9a553c383dab6b0a4a67cee8&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f77911358a1d821f6b1731fac6d2dbd6</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_3.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_22.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_21.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747657208</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Union Institutions">
      <data key="d0">Union Institutions</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Union Institutions are official bodies established under European Union (EU) law, responsible for the deployment, regulation, and oversight of Artificial Intelligence (AI) systems. These institutions may engage in legislative processes and the development of standards related to AI, ensuring cooperation among member states and organizations. Union Institutions encompass various governing bodies within the EU, which oversee regulatory mechanisms and administrative functions. They may also utilize high-risk AI systems in support of law enforcement activities, reflecting their role in governance and regulatory frameworks concerning AI technologies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ba8e0dd4981bd71dedb04a86b45601&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa2b7ae4447e7d43e6030d6793c59ef7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-577494efd5ce842563e6974b0da9707b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f2ba08954f9ed754c2566e3c59b63ee7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c72b31b17ef0accd3df4ab25a063163&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f116a95954c59b63ce3149e6283ed8e</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_59.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_43.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_23.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_21.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_66.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_49.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747657902</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Article 12">
      <data key="d0">Article 12</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Article 12 outlines the documentation and logs required from providers of high-risk AI systems upon request.&lt;SEP&gt;Article 12 pertains to regulations concerning the proper collection, storage, and interpretation of logs related to high-risk AI systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2edb4e37d5b6a1ec68d071ea273b4e52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa2b7ae4447e7d43e6030d6793c59ef7</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_21.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_13.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747464135</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Language">
      <data key="d0">Language</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Language refers to the means of communication that needs to be used by providers to ensure the documentation is understandable to competent authorities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fa2b7ae4447e7d43e6030d6793c59ef7</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_21.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Logs">
      <data key="d0">Logs</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Logs are automatically generated records of the operations and activities of high-risk AI systems, which competent authorities can request to access.&lt;SEP&gt;Logs are records generated by the AI system that track operations and are crucial for audits and compliance with regulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2edb4e37d5b6a1ec68d071ea273b4e52&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa2b7ae4447e7d43e6030d6793c59ef7</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_21.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_13.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747464135</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Generative AI Models">
      <data key="d0">Generative AI Models</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Generative AI models are advanced algorithms capable of producing diverse forms of content including text, audio, images, and video, and are recognized as general-purpose AI models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8033b978339bfb2f89fde823ffa3f524</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_99.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Flexible Content Generation">
      <data key="d0">Flexible Content Generation</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8033b978339bfb2f89fde823ffa3f524</data>
      <data key="d2">Generative AI models exemplify the capability of flexible content generation across multiple media types.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_99.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="experts">
      <data key="d0">experts</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The experts on the scientific panel are individuals with specialized knowledge in AI, selected for their independence and technical competence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f3c3fe390cb5d40b199e1bcc024928f</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_68.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="enforcement activities">
      <data key="d0">enforcement activities</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Enforcement activities refer to the efforts made to implement and regulate general-purpose AI models and systems as defined by the Regulation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f3c3fe390cb5d40b199e1bcc024928f</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_68.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="independent experts">
      <data key="d0">independent experts</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Independent experts are individuals with expertise who are involved in the evaluation process of the general-purpose AI models.&lt;SEP&gt;Independent experts are selected by the Commission for their up-to-date scientific and technical expertise in the AI field.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-075ecb5835777e6044264a23ca0cb20a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f3c3fe390cb5d40b199e1bcc024928f</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_92.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_68.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="tasks">
      <data key="d0">tasks</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Tasks refer to the responsibilities assigned to the scientific panel, including advising on the classification and evaluation of AI systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1f3c3fe390cb5d40b199e1bcc024928f</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_68.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="market surveillance authorities">
      <data key="d0">market surveillance authorities</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Market surveillance authorities are organizations tasked with overseeing market activities to ensure compliance with laws and regulations, particularly in cases of high-risk products.&lt;SEP&gt;Market surveillance authorities are regulatory bodies responsible for ensuring compliance with market regulations and overseeing market conduct.&lt;SEP&gt;Market surveillance authorities are specialized agencies tasked with monitoring and enforcing compliance with established market regulations. Their primary responsibilities include ensuring adherence to regulations, protecting public interests, and overseeing market activities to maintain compliance and integrity, particularly within financial systems. They also play a critical role in overseeing the use of AI systems and models, often seeking advice and support from scientific panels to enhance their oversight capabilities. Furthermore, market surveillance authorities are dedicated to ensuring that products and services in the market meet regulatory and safety standards, thereby reinforcing consumer protection and market integrity.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-83ca16d466deaa751e3b9dc390e91e2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c408a5b914a49af8b42e7e84ad02be6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-82b1b08be1175d2e6d49ab5a294ef86a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75ec68339f2da8ef06976767a0d5b494&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aacbea1ddddc58fbf14c882b36d9a6b3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-78dcdae0f5d22df28405d8ae03e038d0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fdf8a675497acfbeb420b95621b7cb31&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f3c3fe390cb5d40b199e1bcc024928f</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_149.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_26.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_68.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_160.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_65.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_78.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747657948</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Union safeguard procedure">
      <data key="d0">Union safeguard procedure</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Union safeguard procedure is a regulatory process to address systemic risks associated with AI models within the European Union.&lt;SEP&gt;The Union safeguard procedure pertains to processes in ensuring the safe deployment and assessment of AI technologies within the Union.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c408a5b914a49af8b42e7e84ad02be6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f3c3fe390cb5d40b199e1bcc024928f</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_68.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="systemic risks">
      <data key="d0">systemic risks</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Systemic risks are potential threats that could cause widespread negative effects on different systems or stakeholders within a framework.&lt;SEP&gt;Systemic risks refer to potential widespread issues that could arise from the deployment of general-purpose AI models and systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-075ecb5835777e6044264a23ca0cb20a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f3c3fe390cb5d40b199e1bcc024928f</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_92.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_68.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="conflicts of interest">
      <data key="d0">conflicts of interest</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Conflicts of interest are situations where personal interests could interfere with the impartiality required in the tasks performed by the experts.&lt;SEP&gt;Conflicts of interest refer to situations where experts on the scientific panel might have competing interests that could influence their impartiality.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c408a5b914a49af8b42e7e84ad02be6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1f3c3fe390cb5d40b199e1bcc024928f</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_68.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Article 90">
      <data key="d0">Article 90</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Article 90 pertains to regulations involving the evaluation and classification of general-purpose AI models and systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Article 90 specifies provisions for assessing systemic risks associated with AI models and involves alerts from scientific panels.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-23ab4f338bfa06297d4c996d7d54dc70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c408a5b914a49af8b42e7e84ad02be6</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_92.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_68.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Article 74(11)">
      <data key="d0">Article 74(11)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Article 74(11) refers to a specific provision within a regulation that outlines responsibilities for market surveillance activities.&lt;SEP&gt;Article 74(11) relates to activities of cross-border market surveillance and the powers of authorities in this context.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-98532c431ec75ae38c76dc0730337a16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c408a5b914a49af8b42e7e84ad02be6</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_68.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_66.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747464187</data>
    </node>
    <node id="systemic risk">
      <data key="d0">systemic risk</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Systemic risk is the potential for a major disruption in the financial system or market due to the mismanagement of AI models.&lt;SEP&gt;Systemic risk refers to the potential threat posed at the Union level by AI models, which may have widespread implications.&lt;SEP&gt;Systemic risk refers to the potential for a significant disruption or failure in the system as a whole, particularly in the context of AI technologies and their impacts on society.&lt;SEP&gt;Systemic risk pertains to risks posed by high-impact capabilities of AI models that can affect the Union market or societal well-being on a large scale.&lt;SEP&gt;Systemic risk pertains to the potential significant negative effects of high-impact AI capabilities on public health and safety, and their ability to propagate risks across various sectors.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-140dff8b8263721e25ea86a4104c273d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1c408a5b914a49af8b42e7e84ad02be6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8ec7c9ce58b650afe456517c2483e56c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-afea58b87ff95e4731bff6457b0b57c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3266d68b5262e6852cabbe36178dec96</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_3.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_90.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_68.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_113.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747657226</data>
    </node>
    <node id="confidentiality">
      <data key="d0">confidentiality</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Confidentiality refers to the obligation of experts on the scientific panel to keep information obtained during their tasks secure and private.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1c408a5b914a49af8b42e7e84ad02be6</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_68.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Scientific Panel">
      <data key="d0">Scientific Panel</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The Scientific Panel is a body composed of experts who provide vital expertise and support in the development of codes of practice and the evaluation of artificial intelligence (AI) models. Established under Article 68, the panel is tasked with monitoring the capabilities and impact of AI technologies and issuing alerts to the Commission regarding potential systemic risks associated with these models, following specified criteria. Moreover, the panel integrates input from the scientific community into the regulation and governance frameworks surrounding AI, ensuring that scientific perspectives are incorporated in discussions and decisions. Through its various roles, the Scientific Panel aims to promote informed and responsible AI development while addressing the challenges posed by emerging technologies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dbcc9707fb1611da88a5526a5558a165&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c1ef1c9ca92c002cf6ad36264fe1292c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-23ab4f338bfa06297d4c996d7d54dc70&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e519b84a1d0987cd3b0aab32a476aa33&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69b5f24963810305f2a7846d54923bf0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0e6a5cc59273abbe42c743808026477c</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_92.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_148.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_52.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_116.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_51.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_68.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747657838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Members">
      <data key="d0">Members</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Members are individuals who comprise the scientific panel, responsible for issuing alerts and executing tasks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dbcc9707fb1611da88a5526a5558a165</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_68.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Regulatory Compliance">
      <data key="d0">Regulatory Compliance</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Regulatory Compliance refers to the adherence to laws, regulations, guidelines, and specifications relevant to business processes and activities, particularly concerning high-risk AI systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Regulatory compliance involves adhering to laws and regulations that govern the operation and safety of AI systems and products.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ac68c725e087cb7c659f2dd75f0a6e34&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fa2298fd83910d35175065de74977bd4</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_17.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_64.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Design Control">
      <data key="d0">Design Control</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Design Control refers to the systematic procedures used to manage the development and modifications of high-risk AI systems, ensuring they meet regulatory standards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ac68c725e087cb7c659f2dd75f0a6e34</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_17.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="European Union Regulation">
      <data key="d0">European Union Regulation</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">European Union Regulation encompasses the legal framework governing the implementation and monitoring of high-risk AI systems across member states.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c32bc5fa5cdfa35a393a42535dc4fdfb</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_17.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Data Filtration">
      <data key="d0">Data Filtration</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Data Filtration refers to the process of filtering and managing data to ensure its quality and relevance before being applied to high-risk AI systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c32bc5fa5cdfa35a393a42535dc4fdfb</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_17.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Data Mining">
      <data key="d0">Data Mining</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Data Mining is the practice of analyzing and extracting valuable information from large data sets, crucial for the operation of AI systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c32bc5fa5cdfa35a393a42535dc4fdfb</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_17.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Data Aggregation">
      <data key="d0">Data Aggregation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Data Aggregation is the collection and compilation of data from various sources into a single format for analysis and operational purposes in AI.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c32bc5fa5cdfa35a393a42535dc4fdfb</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_17.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Data Retention">
      <data key="d0">Data Retention</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Data Retention involves storing data over a specified period, ensuring compliance with regulations for high-risk AI systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c32bc5fa5cdfa35a393a42535dc4fdfb</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_17.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Customers">
      <data key="d0">Customers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Customers are the end-users or clients who interact with high-risk AI systems, having an interest in the systems' safety and efficacy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c32bc5fa5cdfa35a393a42535dc4fdfb</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_17.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Interested Parties">
      <data key="d0">Interested Parties</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Interested Parties are any stakeholders who have a vested interest in the performance and regulation of high-risk AI systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c32bc5fa5cdfa35a393a42535dc4fdfb</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_17.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Transparency Requirements">
      <data key="d0">Transparency Requirements</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Transparency Requirements pertain to the obligations of AI system providers and deployers to ensure understanding of the system's functionality and risks before it is used.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4a65ec6b0fbd17afe47587686523a725</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_72.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Instructions for Use">
      <data key="d0">Instructions for Use</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Instructions for Use are comprehensive guidelines provided by the Provider that encompass essential information for the safe and effective operation of high-risk AI systems. These instructions detail the characteristics, functionalities, and limitations of the AI systems, aimed at assisting deployers in their implementation. They highlight the obligations and intended uses of the systems, ensuring users understand the proper usage protocols. Specifically, Instructions for Use include critical risk considerations that are vital for safe operation, thereby ensuring users are well-informed about the intended purpose and functionality of the AI systems they are utilizing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4a65ec6b0fbd17afe47587686523a725&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6f5fc2b531499a81e154196ee1e86ac6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-65f69156748df83d9fa179140a3fe2aa&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7dbd41da6ea2a77fff5beb18c8e7c896&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07e8d113bd66975dbb8053a37cc427fc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b2f186d6b95e69680f63fabb65ad924a</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_3.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_65.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_13.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_72.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747657162</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Target Deployers">
      <data key="d0">Target Deployers</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Target Deployers are the intended users of the AI system who must understand the instructions for use and their obligations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-07e8d113bd66975dbb8053a37cc427fc</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_72.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Technical Robustness">
      <data key="d0">Technical Robustness</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Technical Robustness is the quality that high-risk AI systems should possess to ensure they are resilient against errors, faults, and unexpected situations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f15de033aa4361b6dbf4b5d72ede05a5</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_75.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Technical Measures">
      <data key="d0">Technical Measures</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Technical Measures are actions taken to design and develop AI systems to prevent undesirable behavior and ensure their robustness.&lt;SEP&gt;Technical Measures refer to the specific strategies and tools applied by deployers to ensure the proper functionality and compliance of high-risk AI systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-e8c5a915dca180e1c423cefa27ea6cd3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f15de033aa4361b6dbf4b5d72ede05a5</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_26.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_75.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747657948</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Organizational Measures">
      <data key="d0">Organizational Measures</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Organizational Measures involve practices within organizations that enhance the reliability and safety of AI systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f15de033aa4361b6dbf4b5d72ede05a5</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_75.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fail-Safe Plans">
      <data key="d0">Fail-Safe Plans</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Fail-Safe Plans are mechanisms within AI systems that allow for safe interruption of operation during anomalies or boundary breaches.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f15de033aa4361b6dbf4b5d72ede05a5</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_75.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Errors">
      <data key="d0">Errors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Errors refer to mistakes or faults that may occur in AI systems, potentially leading to harmful outcomes if not mitigated.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f15de033aa4361b6dbf4b5d72ede05a5</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_75.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Faults">
      <data key="d0">Faults</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Faults are defects or failures in a system that can disrupt the proper functioning of AI systems, necessitating robust measures to address them.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f15de033aa4361b6dbf4b5d72ede05a5</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_75.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Inconsistencies">
      <data key="d0">Inconsistencies</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Inconsistencies are discrepancies that may arise in the behavior or outputs of AI systems, contributing to risks if not carefully managed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f15de033aa4361b6dbf4b5d72ede05a5</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_75.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Unexpected Situations">
      <data key="d0">Unexpected Situations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Unexpected Situations refer to unforeseen circumstances that can impact the performance of AI systems, requiring built-in safety protocols.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f15de033aa4361b6dbf4b5d72ede05a5</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_75.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Harmful Behavior">
      <data key="d0">Harmful Behavior</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Harmful Behavior encompasses negative actions or outputs resulting from AI systems that can adversely affect users or stakeholders.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f15de033aa4361b6dbf4b5d72ede05a5</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_75.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Undesirable Behavior">
      <data key="d0">Undesirable Behavior</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Undesirable Behavior includes actions or outputs from AI systems that, while not necessarily harmful, may not align with user expectations or ethical standards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f15de033aa4361b6dbf4b5d72ede05a5</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_75.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Data Minimisation">
      <data key="d0">Data Minimisation</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Data minimisation is a principle requiring that only necessary personal data be collected and processed to reduce risks to privacy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d18c3755735a13a436e766a5ca73fc4</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_69.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Data Protection by Design and by Default">
      <data key="d0">Data Protection by Design and by Default</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Data protection by design and by default is a principle aimed at integrating data protection measures into the development of technologies and processes from the outset.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d18c3755735a13a436e766a5ca73fc4</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_69.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anonymisation and Encryption">
      <data key="d0">Anonymisation and Encryption</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Anonymisation and encryption are security measures employed to protect personal data by making it unidentifiable or unreadable.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d18c3755735a13a436e766a5ca73fc4</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_69.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Anonymisation">
      <data key="d0">Anonymisation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Anonymisation is a technique used to remove identifiable information from personal data sets, thereby protecting individuals' identities and privacy.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d18c3755735a13a436e766a5ca73fc4</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_69.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Encryption">
      <data key="d0">Encryption</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Encryption is a method of converting information or data into a code to prevent unauthorized access, ensuring that sensitive data remains confidential during processing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7d18c3755735a13a436e766a5ca73fc4</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_69.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Data Governance">
      <data key="d0">Data Governance</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Data governance refers to the overall management of the availability, usability, integrity, and security of data employed within AI systems, ensuring compliance with relevant regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;Data governance refers to the overall management of the availability, usability, integrity, and security of the data employed in an organization, ensuring compliance with relevant laws and regulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d7eb8204c84c6a7310e75effed716fcf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7d18c3755735a13a436e766a5ca73fc4</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_67.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_69.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747657792</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Third Countries">
      <data key="d0">Third Countries</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Third Countries are nations outside the European Union that enter into international agreements with the Union or Member States for cooperation.&lt;SEP&gt;Third countries are nations outside the Union that do not operate under the Union's legal and regulatory framework but interact with it via market activities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6a90a47461f525bba18d74c42ea04f88&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-873c3732f26f3ace86c77cfec67991ee</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_54.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_22.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="free and open-source licence">
      <data key="d0">free and open-source licence</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Free and open-source licences are legal terms that permit access, usage, modification, and distribution of software, including AI models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b567607dbb431b0d294cd02679760039</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_54.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="provider">
      <data key="d0">provider</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The provider is an entity, which can be a natural or legal person, that has specific obligations under certain regulations, highlighting its role in compliance and accountability. This entity is responsible for developing and managing high-risk AI systems, ensuring that these systems comply with relevant regulations. Additionally, the provider offers or maintains general-purpose AI models and is tasked with ensuring compliance with EU regulations. The provider is also authorized to represent legal persons in matters concerning general-purpose AI models, and generally refers to individuals or organizations that supply AI systems to the market while upholding regulatory standards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8af2de29a8b78dd2b512b6eb64d912a6&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b567607dbb431b0d294cd02679760039&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-610573a7c194ad780ed97782200a740c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19283d37b8085eb0b86aff77d85558de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-075ecb5835777e6044264a23ca0cb20a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c361162e59f587f531364714bb3e3c4c</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_22.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_92.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_54.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_80.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_79.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747658096</data>
    </node>
    <node id="European Digital Innovation Hubs">
      <data key="d0">European Digital Innovation Hubs</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">European Digital Innovation Hubs are entities that play a crucial role in fostering the development and scalability of digital technologies, including artificial intelligence (AI) systems. They are designed to support the digital transformation of businesses, particularly small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs), by providing access to innovation services, funding, and resources. These institutions offer expertise and resources that assist organizations in integrating advanced technologies such as AI and other digital innovations. Additionally, European Digital Innovation Hubs aim to support providers and notified bodies by facilitating compliance with market requirements and delivering specialized knowledge in the field.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dc97d8ad7f3aa0522d885f92ee323822&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ba662546e18e6e5f82c3cd1c5b4c3b75&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7859a0c655f74761757b416c3b50bf36&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8eae051373e28d701c4893438950556b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aca56f82b70892874784bcec5c2bb2c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0271020749ae70ce56cbd607a6f6662a</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_145.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_139.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_58.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_68.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747658106</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AI System Providers">
      <data key="d0">AI System Providers</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">AI System Providers are entities that place AI systems on the market or put them into service, regardless of their location.&lt;SEP&gt;AI system providers are entities that apply for participation in the AI regulatory sandboxes, subject to eligibility and selection criteria.&lt;SEP&gt;AI system providers are entities that develop and supply AI technologies capable of generating or manipulating media content.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-13991bdab077b3114e8913f89a1371e5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4acea71d2f59038573b7ec13db4ad10b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aca56f82b70892874784bcec5c2bb2c3</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_134.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_2.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_58.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747464649</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Relevant Actors">
      <data key="d0">Relevant Actors</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Relevant actors within the AI ecosystem include stakeholders like researchers, testing facilities, and standardization organizations that support the operation of AI regulatory sandboxes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aca56f82b70892874784bcec5c2bb2c3</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_58.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Conformity Assessment Obligations">
      <data key="d0">Conformity Assessment Obligations</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Conformity assessment obligations are requirements that AI system providers must comply with to ensure their systems meet regulatory standards.&lt;SEP&gt;Conformity assessment obligations refer to the requirements that organizations must meet to comply with regulatory standards, particularly in the context of AI systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dc97d8ad7f3aa0522d885f92ee323822&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-aca56f82b70892874784bcec5c2bb2c3</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_58.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Codes of Conduct">
      <data key="d0">Codes of Conduct</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Codes of Conduct are guidelines developed by AI Providers to ensure ethical practices and compliance with regulations in the deployment of AI systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Codes of Conduct serve as guidelines for the ethical development and deployment of AI systems based on established principles.&lt;SEP&gt;Codes of conduct are guidelines established to ensure ethical and safe practices within the AI ecosystem, particularly for SMEs and start-ups in regulatory sandboxes.&lt;SEP&gt;Codes of conduct refer to voluntary guidelines that AI system providers may apply for adherence as part of their participation in the regulatory framework.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-aca56f82b70892874784bcec5c2bb2c3&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-849f8f0403e6b12fa8a9c24b84c094c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc97d8ad7f3aa0522d885f92ee323822&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c14e1756e1d8d71ac3ba07125f122da</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_58.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_27.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_165.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747657078</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Research and Experimentation Labs">
      <data key="d0">Research and Experimentation Labs</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Research and Experimentation Labs are facilities dedicated to the development, testing, and validation of new technologies, including AI systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Research and experimentation labs are facilities focused on the development and testing of new technologies and methodologies in AI research.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dc97d8ad7f3aa0522d885f92ee323822&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7859a0c655f74761757b416c3b50bf36</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_139.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_58.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Start-Ups">
      <data key="d0">Start-Ups</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Start-Ups are newly established businesses, often focused on innovative products or services, and are typically in the early stages of development.&lt;SEP&gt;Start-Ups are small businesses that may develop innovative AI systems and are supported through access to AI regulatory sandboxes to enter the Union market.&lt;SEP&gt;Start-ups are newly established businesses that often focus on innovative products or services and face unique challenges in regulatory compliance, particularly in emerging fields like AI.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dc97d8ad7f3aa0522d885f92ee323822&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-184360d53ae652d7f48a919e168f375a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-34333cde7ca701413cb4d0a0ef1129e1</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_58.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_57.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747657701</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Testing and Experimentation Facilities">
      <data key="d0">Testing and Experimentation Facilities</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Testing and Experimentation Facilities provide environments and systems for rigorously evaluating the performance and compliance of AI technologies.&lt;SEP&gt;Testing and experimentation facilities provide the infrastructure needed to assess and validate new technologies, including AI systems, in controlled environments.&lt;SEP&gt;Testing and experimentation facilities refer to environments where AI systems can be safely tested and assessed for compliance with regulatory standards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dc97d8ad7f3aa0522d885f92ee323822&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7859a0c655f74761757b416c3b50bf36&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0271020749ae70ce56cbd607a6f6662a</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_139.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_58.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Learning Outcomes of AI Regulatory Sandboxes">
      <data key="d0">Learning Outcomes of AI Regulatory Sandboxes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Learning outcomes of AI regulatory sandboxes refer to the insights and knowledge gained from testing AI systems, which can inform compliance and best practices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dc97d8ad7f3aa0522d885f92ee323822</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_58.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Guidance on Implementation">
      <data key="d0">Guidance on Implementation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Guidance on implementation refers to informational resources provided to help organizations comply with laws and regulations, particularly in AI and data protection.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0271020749ae70ce56cbd607a6f6662a</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_58.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Standardisation Documents">
      <data key="d0">Standardisation Documents</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Standardisation documents are official guidelines and protocols that facilitate compliance and consistency in regulatory practices across organizations and industries.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0271020749ae70ce56cbd607a6f6662a</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_58.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Centres of Excellence">
      <data key="d0">Centres of Excellence</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Centres of Excellence are specialized institutions that promote best practices and knowledge transfer in areas such as technology and innovation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0271020749ae70ce56cbd607a6f6662a</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_58.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="authorities">
      <data key="d0">authorities</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The authorities refer to governing bodies or organizations responsible for enforcing laws and regulations within the Union.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ca7de1b4c82ecd24e2aef654437bd0e7</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_58.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Open-Source AI Models">
      <data key="d0">Open-Source AI Models</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Open-source AI models are versions of AI systems that are publicly available for use and modification, which may pose challenges for risk compliance post-release.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1fb0ee5d200ca5b71eb6d9f53ad070ef</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_112.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Training Process">
      <data key="d0">Training Process</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The training process involves allocating compute resources and planning to develop AI models, which is critical in determining their capabilities and risks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1fb0ee5d200ca5b71eb6d9f53ad070ef</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_112.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Compute Resources">
      <data key="d0">Compute Resources</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Compute resources refer to the technical infrastructure (such as CPUs and GPUs) required for the training and operation of AI models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1fb0ee5d200ca5b71eb6d9f53ad070ef</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_112.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Presumption of Systemic Risk">
      <data key="d0">Presumption of Systemic Risk</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The presumption of systemic risk occurs when a general-purpose AI model meets certain thresholds indicating high-impact capabilities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1fb0ee5d200ca5b71eb6d9f53ad070ef</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_112.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="open-source model">
      <data key="d0">open-source model</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The open-source model refers to a software development approach where the source code is made publicly available for use, modification, and distribution.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c087216cbefba4022c0cdbe838e30f19</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_112.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Compliance Measures">
      <data key="d0">Compliance Measures</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Compliance measures are procedures and actions taken to ensure adherence to regulations and laws.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-c087216cbefba4022c0cdbe838e30f19</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_112.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Conformity Assessment Procedures">
      <data key="d0">Conformity Assessment Procedures</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Conformity Assessment Procedures are evaluative processes to ensure that high-risk AI systems meet established safety, health, and environmental standards before they can be authorized.&lt;SEP&gt;Conformity Assessment Procedures refer to the processes involved in evaluating whether AI systems comply with established regulations, particularly those deemed high-risk.&lt;SEP&gt;Conformity Assessment Procedures are processes that ensure products comply with specific requirements before they can be sold in the market.&lt;SEP&gt;Conformity Assessment Procedures are methods and standards that ensure AI systems meet required specifications before certification.&lt;SEP&gt;Conformity assessment procedures are systematic processes outlined in regulations to evaluate the compliance of high-risk AI systems with established standards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d1d974005cd87fc21678c732472ee11a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e07ee6b3452d764ccd9733dceadb1ac7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9b7fd511ed97ef1a87c9783c3425b7d4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-397d6e2474c4f45e883f265653f2e715&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98532c431ec75ae38c76dc0730337a16</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_46.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_44.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_66.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_34.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_173.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747657631</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Commission Expert Groups">
      <data key="d0">Commission Expert Groups</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Commission expert groups are gatherings of experts involved in the preparation of delegated acts for the European Union.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6001fd4ce94d732804de396c3240c65f</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_173.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Expert Groups">
      <data key="d0">Expert Groups</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Expert Groups are collaborative bodies comprised of experts who assist in the preparation and evaluation of delegated acts for the European Commission.&lt;SEP&gt;Expert Groups consist of professionals providing advice and developing expertise in specific fields, such as product safety and cybersecurity within the Union context.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6001fd4ce94d732804de396c3240c65f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-577494efd5ce842563e6974b0da9707b</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_173.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_66.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747464195</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Regulation for AI Models">
      <data key="d0">Regulation for AI Models</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Regulation for AI Models outlines specific obligations and rules governing the provision and integration of general-purpose AI models within the market.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-161f3120961644fbdc17c7b09b46e54b</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_97.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Developers of AI Models">
      <data key="d0">Developers of AI Models</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Developers of AI Models are responsible for integrating general-purpose AI models into systems and must comply with regulations when these models are marketed.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-161f3120961644fbdc17c7b09b46e54b</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_97.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Obligations for Providers of AI Models">
      <data key="d0">Obligations for Providers of AI Models</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Obligations for Providers of AI Models refer to the legal responsibilities that developers must adhere to when placing their models in the market.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-161f3120961644fbdc17c7b09b46e54b</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_97.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Components of AI Systems">
      <data key="d0">Components of AI Systems</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Components of AI Systems include all necessary elements that work together with AI models to create functional AI applications.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-161f3120961644fbdc17c7b09b46e54b</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_97.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="User Interface">
      <data key="d0">User Interface</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The User Interface is a crucial component that allows users to interact with AI systems, enhancing usability and accessibility.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-161f3120961644fbdc17c7b09b46e54b</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_97.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Systemic Risks of AI Models">
      <data key="d0">Systemic Risks of AI Models</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Systemic Risks of AI Models refer to potential negative consequences that could arise from the deployment of certain AI models in the market.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-161f3120961644fbdc17c7b09b46e54b</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_97.json</data>
