      <data key="d5">1747657728</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Data Processing Environment">
      <data key="d0">Data Processing Environment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Data processing environment is an isolated and secure context in which personal data can be handled according to specific regulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-800752b66b6fe0cd70bb0290efc2fbbe</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_59.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Law Enforcement Authorities">
      <data key="d0">Law Enforcement Authorities</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Law Enforcement Authorities are governmental agencies tasked with maintaining public order, enforcing laws, and ensuring the safety and rights of citizens. They play a critical role in preventing, detecting, investigating, and prosecuting criminal offenses. As part of their operations, these authorities may utilize high-risk AI systems to enhance their capabilities, although such systems are not covered under post-market monitoring obligations due to their operational sensitivity. Additionally, Law Enforcement Authorities may operate within AI regulatory sandboxes, enabling them to incorporate advanced technologies while adhering to regulatory standards. Overall, these agencies are integral to fostering public safety and order through a combination of traditional law enforcement practices and the innovative use of technology.&lt;SEP&gt;Law enforcement authorities are agencies involved in the application or use of high-risk AI systems in public safety.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-77ba8e0dd4981bd71dedb04a86b45601&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bbcf833252ce65fd6a1d8bd1f5a6efc1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f56b40e98f8949a37c3ab283b01f7050&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a23e03a78af2035b69e69bd4fc65a435&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-69838403e8b59bb122436a0d25a9fc1e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-800752b66b6fe0cd70bb0290efc2fbbe&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-878d589bc7b69ae2d9c3a44284a1badc</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_33.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_130.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_72.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_59.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_59.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_43.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747657900</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Testing Results">
      <data key="d0">Testing Results</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Testing results are the outcomes derived from the evaluation of AI systems in the sandbox, providing evidence of their development and compliance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-800752b66b6fe0cd70bb0290efc2fbbe</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_59.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AI regulatory sandboxes">
      <data key="d0">AI regulatory sandboxes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">AI regulatory sandboxes are controlled environments created by regulators to allow companies, particularly SMEs and start-ups, to test innovative AI systems under regulatory oversight before full-scale implementation.&lt;SEP&gt;AI regulatory sandboxes are frameworks established for the testing and validation of AI systems under controlled conditions, ensuring compliance with relevant laws and regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;AI regulatory sandboxes are frameworks that allow for the trial of AI technologies in a controlled environment to ensure compliance with regulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3d6ed86abb2e8f0e12897e923d1e9964&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-98532c431ec75ae38c76dc0730337a16&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2612abd3c26dca69a0acec30add3ef15</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_59.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_143.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_66.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747464772</data>
    </node>
    <node id="law enforcement authorities">
      <data key="d0">law enforcement authorities</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Law enforcement authorities are agencies tasked with maintaining public order and enforcing laws.&lt;SEP&gt;Law enforcement authorities are governmental agencies responsible for the prevention and investigation of criminal offences and the enforcement of laws regarding public security.&lt;SEP&gt;Law enforcement authorities are mandated agencies responsible for the prevention and investigation of crime, enforcement of the law, and maintenance of public order.&lt;SEP&gt;Law enforcement authorities are governmental agencies responsible for the enforcement of laws, preventing crime, and maintaining public order.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a23e03a78af2035b69e69bd4fc65a435&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-75ec68339f2da8ef06976767a0d5b494&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2612abd3c26dca69a0acec30add3ef15&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4882abf1dd869d3dff00b8d88ee946a8</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_33.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_26.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_78.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_59.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747657974</data>
    </node>
    <node id="personal data">
      <data key="d0">personal data</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Personal data encompasses any information that relates to an identified or identifiable individual, subject to data protection regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;Personal data refers to any information that relates to an identified or identifiable individual, particularly in the context of AI systems and their regulatory oversight.&lt;SEP&gt;Personal data refers to any data relating to identified or identifiable individuals, which is protected under various regulations including those mentioned.&lt;SEP&gt;Personal data refers to any information relating to an identified or identifiable person, which is protected under related regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;Personal data is defined under EU regulation as information that relates to an identified or identifiable individual.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-cfc036b3a79fdcd1eb36c0af6aafbb4d&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0373d38f7600f51def3b5e5cb5061d17&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f8833999c9ef9aa31a007f2e77ffea02&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3105f8ed20ed5608a4571fe7b1d8f942&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2612abd3c26dca69a0acec30add3ef15</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_3.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_2.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_59.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_50.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747657205</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Criminal Offences">
      <data key="d0">Criminal Offences</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Criminal Offences entail violations of laws that require investigation or prosecution, often necessitating the processing of personal data for law enforcement purposes.&lt;SEP&gt;Criminal Offences refer to illegal activities that may invoke the law, necessitating the deployment of AI systems for prevention and investigation.&lt;SEP&gt;Criminal offences refer to illegal activities that may require detection or intervention by AI systems under certain legal frameworks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2bccefc944ad0794c75bc69b977c88a1&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50cb89ed7d0d818018d373ebefa65267&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2612abd3c26dca69a0acec30add3ef15</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_59.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_50.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747464570</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Processing of Personal Data">
      <data key="d0">Processing of Personal Data</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Processing of Personal Data involves handling and analyzing data related to individuals, which is subject to legal standards in the context of AI and law enforcement activities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-2612abd3c26dca69a0acec30add3ef15</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_59.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="natural person">
      <data key="d0">natural person</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A natural person is a human being, as opposed to a legal entity, involved in the application of regulations and laws.&lt;SEP&gt;A natural person refers to an individual human being recognized by law, and in this context, it implies that such individuals can be responsible for high-risk AI systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Natural persons refer to individual human beings with legal rights and obligations.&lt;SEP&gt;A natural person refers to an individual human being who can hold editorial responsibilities for published content.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fdf8a675497acfbeb420b95621b7cb31&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19283d37b8085eb0b86aff77d85558de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f09b5ce9e149c51cfde2a77cd90759&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb040a8b4e6e3fa6f26776f560b6591c</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_79.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_78.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_170.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_134.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747464278</data>
    </node>
    <node id="legal person">
      <data key="d0">legal person</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">A legal person is an entity, such as a corporation or organization, that has legal rights and responsibilities distinct from those of a natural person, which can also be responsible for high-risk AI systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Legal persons refer to entities such as corporations or organizations that have legal rights and responsibilities.&lt;SEP&gt;A legal person represents an organization or a corporation that has legal rights and obligations under the law.&lt;SEP&gt;A legal person refers to a non-human entity, such as a corporation or organization, that has legal rights and responsibilities, including editorial control.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-fdf8a675497acfbeb420b95621b7cb31&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-19283d37b8085eb0b86aff77d85558de&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-03f09b5ce9e149c51cfde2a77cd90759&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb040a8b4e6e3fa6f26776f560b6591c</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_79.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_78.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_170.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_134.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747464278</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Public Entity">
      <data key="d0">Public Entity</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Public Entity describes entities designated by Member States to perform specific tasks related to the supervision and application of the Regulation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1dbe8a4749b15b91fcb1062fee4fa778</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_153.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Recital 29">
      <data key="d0">Recital 29</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Recital 29 outlines prohibitions regarding AI practices that manipulate or deceive individuals, causing significant harm.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6ef0a2a22402771842f4d35bbf4be08d</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Recital 31">
      <data key="d0">Recital 31</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Recital 31 addresses the prohibitions related to the evaluation and classification of individuals based on social behavior or characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6ef0a2a22402771842f4d35bbf4be08d</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Recital 42">
      <data key="d0">Recital 42</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Recital 42 discusses the restrictions on using AI systems for making risk assessments based on profiling individuals for criminal activities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6ef0a2a22402771842f4d35bbf4be08d</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Subliminal Techniques">
      <data key="d0">Subliminal Techniques</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Subliminal techniques refer to methods that operate below the threshold of conscious awareness, often used in marketing or persuasion that manipulate behavior.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6ef0a2a22402771842f4d35bbf4be08d</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Manipulative Techniques">
      <data key="d0">Manipulative Techniques</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Manipulative techniques involve strategies intentionally designed to distort perceptions or compel decisions, often causing harm to individuals or groups.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6ef0a2a22402771842f4d35bbf4be08d</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Vulnerabilities of Natural Persons">
      <data key="d0">Vulnerabilities of Natural Persons</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Vulnerabilities of natural persons are specific weaknesses that can be exploited due to age, disability, or socio-economic status, often leading to harmful outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6ef0a2a22402771842f4d35bbf4be08d</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Social Behavior">
      <data key="d0">Social Behavior</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Social behavior entails actions performed by individuals or groups that can influence their interactions and treatment within society.&lt;SEP&gt;Social behavior refers to actions that individuals engage in when interacting with others, which can influence their social scoring and treatment by AI systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-83317eda1161cf5d13ef9aa07297c98b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6ef0a2a22402771842f4d35bbf4be08d</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Criminal Offence Risk Assessment">
      <data key="d0">Criminal Offence Risk Assessment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Risk assessment concerning criminal offenses involves evaluating the likelihood that an individual may engage in illegal activities based on certain profiling characteristics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-6ef0a2a22402771842f4d35bbf4be08d</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Facial Recognition Databases">
      <data key="d0">Facial Recognition Databases</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Facial recognition databases are collections of facial images used by AI systems for identification, often raised for concerns regarding privacy and consent.&lt;SEP&gt;Facial recognition databases are collections of facial images used for identification purposes, often raising concerns regarding privacy and surveillance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a46d97529092ce970fc3194f0ff15f99&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-83317eda1161cf5d13ef9aa07297c98b</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_43.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747657609</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Biometric Categorisation Systems">
      <data key="d0">Biometric Categorisation Systems</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Biometric Categorisation Systems are used to categorize individuals based on biometric data, like facial recognition, to infer personal attributes.&lt;SEP&gt;Biometric categorisation systems are technologies that analyze biometric data to classify individuals, potentially infringing on personal rights.&lt;SEP&gt;Biometric categorisation systems utilize biometric data, such as faces or fingerprints, to classify individuals based on sensitive attributes like political opinions or sexual orientation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-83317eda1161cf5d13ef9aa07297c98b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c3070277451614bdc67954ec8735dd80&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1be166c61f466e23c3d8c143f6efd3bb</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_30.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Workplace and Education Institutions">
      <data key="d0">Workplace and Education Institutions</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Workplace and Education Institutions represent environments where AI systems may be applied for monitoring and assessing individuals but are subject to strict regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;Workplace and education institutions are environments where AI systems may be used to infer emotions, raising concerns about privacy and ethical implications.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-83317eda1161cf5d13ef9aa07297c98b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1be166c61f466e23c3d8c143f6efd3bb</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Unfavorable Treatment">
      <data key="d0">Unfavorable Treatment</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Unfavorable treatment describes the negative consequences faced by individuals based on their social scores or profiling, impacting their social interactions.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-83317eda1161cf5d13ef9aa07297c98b</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="CCTV Footage">
      <data key="d0">CCTV Footage</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">CCTV Footage consists of recorded video surveillance that captures events in public spaces, often used for security and monitoring.&lt;SEP&gt;CCTV footage is video surveillance that can be used to create or expand facial recognition databases, raising concerns about privacy and consent.&lt;SEP&gt;CCTV footage consists of video recordings captured by closed-circuit television systems, often used for security and surveillance purposes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1be166c61f466e23c3d8c143f6efd3bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a46d97529092ce970fc3194f0ff15f99&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-83317eda1161cf5d13ef9aa07297c98b</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_43.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747657609</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Human Rights">
      <data key="d0">Human Rights</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Human Rights encompass the basic rights and freedoms that belong to every person, which can be affected by the use of AI systems and biometric data.&lt;SEP&gt;Human rights encompass the fundamental rights and freedoms that belong to every person, which can be threatened by AI systems and profiling.&lt;SEP&gt;Human Rights refer to the principles that protect individual freedoms and ensure that AI systems do not infringe upon these rights.&lt;SEP&gt;Human rights refer to the fundamental rights and freedoms that every individual is entitled to, which can be jeopardized by misuse of AI technologies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1be166c61f466e23c3d8c143f6efd3bb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-849f8f0403e6b12fa8a9c24b84c094c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-392a0705db576351b9cbccd6dcfdf33f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-83317eda1161cf5d13ef9aa07297c98b</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_110.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_5.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_27.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747657459</data>
    </node>
    <node id="EU Regulation 2016/679">
      <data key="d0">EU Regulation 2016/679</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">EU Regulation 2016/679 (GDPR) governs the handling of personal data within the European Union, including biometric data for law enforcement purposes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1be166c61f466e23c3d8c143f6efd3bb</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Facial Images">
      <data key="d0">Facial Images</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Facial Images refer to pictures or visual representations of faces that can be collected from various sources, including the internet and CCTV footage, for analysis and identification.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1be166c61f466e23c3d8c143f6efd3bb</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Internet">
      <data key="d0">Internet</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The Internet is a global network that facilitates the exchange of information and the sharing of data, including facial images, which can be scraped for various purposes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1be166c61f466e23c3d8c143f6efd3bb</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Emotional Inference">
      <data key="d0">Emotional Inference</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Emotional Inference is the process by which AI systems interpret and deduce human emotions based on analysis of data, particularly facial expressions and biometric indicators.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1be166c61f466e23c3d8c143f6efd3bb</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Abolition of Biometric Systems">
      <data key="d0">Abolition of Biometric Systems</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Abolition of Biometric Systems refers to discussions or regulations aiming to limit or prohibit the use of biometric categorization technologies due to privacy and ethical concerns.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1be166c61f466e23c3d8c143f6efd3bb</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Human Beings">
      <data key="d0">Human Beings</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Human Beings are individuals whose biometric data can be collected and analyzed by AI systems, often leading to privacy implications and questions of consent.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-1be166c61f466e23c3d8c143f6efd3bb</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="real-time remote biometric identification systems">
      <data key="d0">real-time remote biometric identification systems</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Real-time remote biometric identification systems are technologies used for recognizing and verifying individuals' identities in public spaces for law enforcement purposes.&lt;SEP&gt;These systems are technology used for identifying individuals in real-time in publicly accessible spaces, specifically for law enforcement purposes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0073df6368131f4f385a66430ef1c4f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-239d42cf353b4be7fdeade1f3fced85d</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_34.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="criminal offence">
      <data key="d0">criminal offence</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Criminal offences are actions or behaviors that violate a law and are punishable by statutory law.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0073df6368131f4f385a66430ef1c4f4</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Annex II">
      <data key="d0">Annex II</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Annex II refers to a specific section of regulatory legislation that outlines offenses punishable by custodial sentences that are relevant to law enforcement and biometric processing.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0073df6368131f4f385a66430ef1c4f4</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="terrorist attack">
      <data key="d0">terrorist attack</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Terrorist attacks represent acts of violence against civilians intentionally carried out to instill fear and achieve specific political objectives.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0073df6368131f4f385a66430ef1c4f4</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="criminal investigation">
      <data key="d0">criminal investigation</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Criminal investigation is a legal process conducted to uncover and gather evidence surrounding offenses committed by individuals.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0073df6368131f4f385a66430ef1c4f4</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="crimes">
      <data key="d0">crimes</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Crimes are actions or behaviors that violate laws and are punishable by the state; they include various offenses as defined by legislation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0073df6368131f4f385a66430ef1c4f4</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="criminal penalty">
      <data key="d0">criminal penalty</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A criminal penalty is a consequence assigned by a legal system in response to a criminal act, which can include imprisonment, fines, or other forms of punishment.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0073df6368131f4f385a66430ef1c4f4</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="system">
      <data key="d0">system</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The 'system' in this context refers to the procedural frameworks utilized by law enforcement to carry out investigations and enforce laws regarding biometric data.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0073df6368131f4f385a66430ef1c4f4</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="fundamental rights impact assessment">
      <data key="d0">fundamental rights impact assessment</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">A fundamental rights impact assessment is a process that evaluates the effects of actions or policies on individual rights and freedoms before implementation.&lt;SEP&gt;Fundamental rights impact assessment is a process required to evaluate the effects of using biometric identification systems on individuals' rights and freedoms.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0073df6368131f4f385a66430ef1c4f4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-239d42cf353b4be7fdeade1f3fced85d</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_34.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Law Enforcement Authority">
      <data key="d0">Law Enforcement Authority</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Law Enforcement Authorities are agencies tasked with maintaining public order and enforcing the law, which would utilize the remote biometric identification system under strict regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;The Law Enforcement Authority is a government body responsible for enforcing laws and regulations, including those that govern the use of biometric identification systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc59c087d680631c2a594779449f195&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-dc0db03f57b007cfc458657e88e273ae</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_35.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747464271</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Judicial Authority">
      <data key="d0">Judicial Authority</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A Judicial Authority is an independent body responsible for making legal decisions and granting authorizations related to law enforcement actions, including the use of biometric identification systems.&lt;SEP&gt;The Judicial Authority provides legal oversight and must grant authorization for the use of real-time biometric systems in public spaces, ensuring compliance with national laws.&lt;SEP&gt;Judicial authorities are institutions responsible for upholding the rule of law, making legal decisions, and administering justice.&lt;SEP&gt;Judicial Authority refers to entities within the legal system that possess the power to make decisions on the legality of the use of AI systems in investigations.&lt;SEP&gt;Judicial Authority refers to the body that grants authorization for the use of high-risk AI systems in investigations, with decisions subject to judicial review.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-dc0db03f57b007cfc458657e88e273ae&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffc59c087d680631c2a594779449f195&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-543cc911c9d837b54a06d5f6ca958b34&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9bece07e296fce9647a40a0dde904fe4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4988a00689afb1245e0f0ce4ceef5921</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_61.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_35.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_5.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_26.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747657969</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Real-Time Remote Biometric Identification System">
      <data key="d0">Real-Time Remote Biometric Identification System</data>
      <data key="d1">system</data>
      <data key="d2">This system enables the real-time identification of individuals through biometric data in public spaces, mandated by specific legal frameworks for security purposes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc59c087d680631c2a594779449f195</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Fundamental Rights Impact Assessment">
      <data key="d0">Fundamental Rights Impact Assessment</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Fundamental Rights Impact Assessment refers to the evaluation process to assess how a project or action might impact individuals' fundamental rights.&lt;SEP&gt;The Fundamental Rights Impact Assessment is a legal process that evaluates the potential impact of biometric identification systems on individual rights and freedoms before their implementation.&lt;SEP&gt;The fundamental rights impact assessment is a process to evaluate risks to individuals' rights due to the use of high-risk AI systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3a1d78baf29c07c65edc311f0ca04126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffc59c087d680631c2a594779449f195&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a3f4621c3d73dca675e991da5d0c9317</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_96.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_5.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_27.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747657257</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Real-Time Remote Biometric Identification Systems">
      <data key="d0">Real-Time Remote Biometric Identification Systems</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Real-Time Remote Biometric Identification Systems are technological systems used for identifying individuals' biometrics remotely, proposed to be used under strict regulations in law enforcement.&lt;SEP&gt;Real-Time Remote Biometric Identification Systems are technologies used for identifying individuals in public spaces by law enforcement.&lt;SEP&gt;Real-Time Remote Biometric Identification Systems refer to technologies that utilize biometric data to identify individuals in real time, particularly used in law enforcement scenarios.&lt;SEP&gt;Real-time remote biometric identification systems are technologies used by law enforcement for identifying individuals in public spaces based on biometric data.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50be48c46c67191e901c756873069fcb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-911571ee28a1070a8fcc503dd5d91e14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d91755cda6acde2b40257e6d4cef1695&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ffc59c087d680631c2a594779449f195</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_33.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Seriousness of Harm">
      <data key="d0">Seriousness of Harm</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Seriousness of Harm relates to the potential consequences and risks associated with the use of systems that could affect individual rights and freedoms, particularly in law enforcement contexts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc59c087d680631c2a594779449f195</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Probability of Harm">
      <data key="d0">Probability of Harm</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Probability of Harm assesses the likelihood that harm may occur as a result of the deployment of remote biometric identification systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc59c087d680631c2a594779449f195</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Scale of Harm">
      <data key="d0">Scale of Harm</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Scale of Harm evaluates the extent of potential damage or adverse effects on individuals or communities from the use of biometric identification systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc59c087d680631c2a594779449f195</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Safeguards and Conditions">
      <data key="d0">Safeguards and Conditions</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Safeguards and Conditions are protective measures that ensure the responsible use of biometric identification systems, balancing law enforcement needs with individual rights.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc59c087d680631c2a594779449f195</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Article 27">
      <data key="d0">Article 27</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Article 27 outlines the requirements for conducting a Fundamental Rights Impact Assessment before using biometric identification systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ffc59c087d680631c2a594779449f195</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="real-time remote biometric identification system">
      <data key="d0">real-time remote biometric identification system</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A real-time remote biometric identification system is a technological solution utilized for identifying individuals in publicly accessible spaces, subject to legal regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;The real-time remote biometric identification system is a technology used for identifying individuals in publicly accessible spaces, particularly for law enforcement purposes.&lt;SEP&gt;A type of remote biometric identification system that performs identification instantly or with minimal delays to prevent circumvention.&lt;SEP&gt;This system identifies individuals almost instantaneously by processing biometric data through a comparison system, allowing for minimal delays.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-a94885e0b736d24263914e9a9d565900&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4249f491e5466803d0f267a67c5cb39&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1e726a1ef8f481d54c995afc6a028f60&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-27c28ab4482e4bec149657c656192c46</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_3.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747657195</data>
    </node>
    <node id="judicial authority">
      <data key="d0">judicial authority</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">A judicial authority is the legal body responsible for granting authorization for the use of biometric identification systems, ensuring adherence to legal standards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4249f491e5466803d0f267a67c5cb39</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="independent administrative authority">
      <data key="d0">independent administrative authority</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">An independent administrative authority is a body not influenced by other governmental branches that also has the power to authorize law enforcement use of biometric identification systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4249f491e5466803d0f267a67c5cb39</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="national data protection authority">
      <data key="d0">national data protection authority</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The national data protection authority is an organization in each Member State that regulates the use of data protection laws, particularly relating to biometric identification.&lt;SEP&gt;The national data protection authority is responsible for overseeing the protection of personal data and privacy rights related to the operations of biometric systems.&lt;SEP&gt;The national data protection authority oversees the enforcement of data protection laws and regulations within a member state.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27c28ab4482e4bec149657c656192c46&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4249f491e5466803d0f267a67c5cb39&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4882abf1dd869d3dff00b8d88ee946a8</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_26.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747657974</data>
    </node>
    <node id="publicly accessible spaces">
      <data key="d0">publicly accessible spaces</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">Publicly accessible spaces are areas open to the public where identification systems may be deployed for safety and security purposes.&lt;SEP&gt;Publicly accessible spaces refer to areas where individuals can freely enter, such as streets and parks, that may be monitored by biometric identification systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4249f491e5466803d0f267a67c5cb39&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-239d42cf353b4be7fdeade1f3fced85d</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_34.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="authorization">
      <data key="d0">authorization</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Authorization is the legal process by which permissions are granted for the use of biometric identification systems, contingent on compliance with established legal frameworks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4249f491e5466803d0f267a67c5cb39</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="data retention policies">
      <data key="d0">data retention policies</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Data retention policies govern how long data collected from biometric identification systems can be stored, ensuring compliance with privacy regulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-b4249f491e5466803d0f267a67c5cb39</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="national law">
      <data key="d0">national law</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">National law comprises the legal statutes and regulations enacted by a country to regulate various activities, including the deployment of AI systems.&lt;SEP&gt;National law refers to the legal framework within a Member State that regulates the use of biometric systems and establishes protocols for privacy and data protection.&lt;SEP&gt;National law embodies the legal frameworks and regulations established at the country level to protect rights and freedoms.&lt;SEP&gt;National law includes the legal statutes and regulations set by individual countries, which may vary from Union law, particularly regarding data use.&lt;SEP&gt;National law encompasses the legal framework and statutes that govern a nation's territory and citizens, ensuring protection of intellectual property rights and confidential information.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-071372b252509d0e29e64264c0511359&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-fe4d7c8b1c65cb7b9f5021681b13c271&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c8721c261d628c538814f61ffe3cec22&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-bb040a8b4e6e3fa6f26776f560b6591c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-b4249f491e5466803d0f267a67c5cb39</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_35.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_170.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_52.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_5.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_91.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747657418</data>
    </node>
    <node id="national rules">
      <data key="d0">national rules</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">National rules refer to the legislative measures that Member States must establish regarding the use of biometric identification systems in compliance with Union law.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27c28ab4482e4bec149657c656192c46</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="paragraph 1, first subparagraph, point (h)">
      <data key="d0">paragraph 1, first subparagraph, point (h)</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Paragraph 1, first subparagraph, point (h) outlines the limitations and requirements for the use of biometric identification systems by authorities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27c28ab4482e4bec149657c656192c46</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Recitals 35, 36, 37, 38, 39, 40, and 41">
      <data key="d0">Recitals 35, 36, 37, 38, 39, 40, and 41</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Recitals 35-41 refer to sections of legislation that provide context and rationale for the rules regarding the use of biometric identification systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-27c28ab4482e4bec149657c656192c46</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Member Market Surveillance Authorities">
      <data key="d0">Member Market Surveillance Authorities</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">National market surveillance authorities are bodies responsible for monitoring market compliance with laws related to consumer protection and market regulation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50be48c46c67191e901c756873069fcb</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="National Data Protection Authorities">
      <data key="d0">National Data Protection Authorities</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">National Data Protection Authorities are governmental entities that oversee the compliance of data protection laws, particularly related to personal data processing in AI systems.&lt;SEP&gt;National Data Protection Authorities oversee compliance with data protection laws and regulations, especially in relation to AI systems processing personal data.&lt;SEP&gt;National data protection authorities are bodies responsible for enforcing data protection laws and regulations in their respective countries.&lt;SEP&gt;These are independent public authorities in Member States that protect personal data and privacy, ensuring compliance with data protection laws.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bc038db2f25d511bf84cccc9550e10c2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-184360d53ae652d7f48a919e168f375a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50be48c46c67191e901c756873069fcb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-34333cde7ca701413cb4d0a0ef1129e1</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_39.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_5.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_57.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747657701</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Paragraph 1">
      <data key="d0">Paragraph 1</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Paragraph 1 refers to a specific section of the regulation outlining rules regarding remote biometric identification systems and their legal framework.&lt;SEP&gt;Paragraph 1 refers to the section of the document that outlines essential information required under legal acts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50be48c46c67191e901c756873069fcb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-55bfb9287798720e37fcd8bae961e88b</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_11.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747464090</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Paragraph 2">
      <data key="d0">Paragraph 2</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Paragraph 2 discusses national law requirements for the use of biometric identification systems by competent authorities.&lt;SEP&gt;Paragraph 2 indicates a specific section within Article 98 that outlines particular issues related to implementing acts and their examination.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bec338e77f01240f23cac5480d2c8ae4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-50be48c46c67191e901c756873069fcb</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_56.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Paragraph 4">
      <data key="d0">Paragraph 4</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Paragraph 4 relates to the notification process for authorities to communicate their rules to the Commission.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50be48c46c67191e901c756873069fcb</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Recitals 37, 38, 39, 40, and 41">
      <data key="d0">Recitals 37, 38, 39, 40, and 41</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">These recitals provide context and additional details regarding the regulation of biometric identification systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50be48c46c67191e901c756873069fcb</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Data Protection Authorities">
      <data key="d0">Data Protection Authorities</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Data Protection Authorities are entities responsible for overseeing compliance with data protection laws, ensuring that personal data is handled according to legal standards.&lt;SEP&gt;Data Protection Authorities are specialized governmental bodies that oversee compliance with data protection regulations, safeguarding individuals’ data privacy rights.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-911571ee28a1070a8fcc503dd5d91e14&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2315cd7abb88dee440f081de5430a32f</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_157.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Judicial Authorities">
      <data key="d0">Judicial Authorities</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Judicial Authorities refer to the competent bodies that have the power to make binding decisions regarding the authorization of practices involving data or surveillance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-911571ee28a1070a8fcc503dd5d91e14</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Independent Administrative Authority">
      <data key="d0">Independent Administrative Authority</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Independent Administrative Authorities make binding decisions related to regulatory practices and often operate with a level of independence from government influence.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-911571ee28a1070a8fcc503dd5d91e14</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AI Practice">
      <data key="d0">AI Practice</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">AI Practice encompasses various applications of artificial intelligence, which must adhere to legal constraints in their operations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-911571ee28a1070a8fcc503dd5d91e14</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Annual Reports on Biometric Systems">
      <data key="d0">Annual Reports on Biometric Systems</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Annual Reports on Biometric Systems are comprehensive documents that assess and report on the usage of biometric technologies in public law enforcement.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-911571ee28a1070a8fcc503dd5d91e14</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Paragraphs 1, 2, 3">
      <data key="d0">Paragraphs 1, 2, 3</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-50be48c46c67191e901c756873069fcb</data>
      <data key="d2">The recitals provide necessary context and justification for the rules outlined in paragraphs 1, 2, and 3, influencing the overall regulation framework.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_5.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Compliance Regulation">
      <data key="d0">Compliance Regulation</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The compliance regulation sets out the rules and requirements for AI system providers to ensure their products meet the necessary legal standards in the Union.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fc29985a43f3bd521b3a1c9eb9ba756</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_82.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Required Information">
      <data key="d0">Required Information</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Required information encompasses all data and documentation that operators must provide to ensure compliance with regulations in the Union.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fc29985a43f3bd521b3a1c9eb9ba756</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_82.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Written Mandate">
      <data key="d0">Written Mandate</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The written mandate is a formal document that appoints an authorised representative for compliance purposes within the Union.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3fc29985a43f3bd521b3a1c9eb9ba756</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_82.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Representative">
      <data key="d0">Representative</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Representative is an individual designated by each Member State to participate in the Board's activities.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7e28e08bfb7b1ae3208e2c91ea6f1e4e</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_65.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Three Years">
      <data key="d0">Three Years</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The period of three years refers to the term length for which representatives are appointed to the Board, renewable once.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7e28e08bfb7b1ae3208e2c91ea6f1e4e</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_65.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Sub-groups">
      <data key="d0">Sub-groups</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Sub-groups are specialized groups established by the Board for cooperation and exchange among market surveillance authorities on specific topics.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7e28e08bfb7b1ae3208e2c91ea6f1e4e</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_65.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Chair">
      <data key="d0">Chair</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The Chair is a representative from the Member States responsible for leading the Board and coordinating its activities.&lt;SEP&gt;The Chair is the individual responsible for leading the Board's activities and ensuring effective operation of meetings.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7e28e08bfb7b1ae3208e2c91ea6f1e4e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-83ca16d466deaa751e3b9dc390e91e2f</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_65.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stakeholders">
      <data key="d0">Stakeholders</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">Stakeholders are individuals or groups that possess a vested interest or concern in the development, deployment, and regulation of AI systems. This diverse group includes a wide range of parties such as providers and users of AI technology, industry representatives, national authorities, market participants, academia, civil society, and various advocacy groups, particularly small and medium-sized enterprises (SMEs), consumer organizations, and environmental advocates. 

Stakeholders play a crucial role in the standardization of AI practices and are instrumental in promoting ethical considerations in the development of AI technologies. They engage actively in the implementation and application of regulations, especially concerning high-risk AI systems, where their interests encompass not only organizations and users but also regulatory bodies. Furthermore, stakeholders participate in AI regulatory sandboxes, collaborating with businesses, innovators, and regulatory entities to explore and implement AI technologies effectively.

Additionally, stakeholders include experts who are invited to partake in forums and meetings related to AI governance and development, thus ensuring that the implementation of AI policies and regulations reflects diverse perspectives and interests from across all sectors of society. Overall, stakeholders are integral to the ongoing dialogue around AI, involved in shaping and implementing standards and policies that govern AI technologies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0e6a5cc59273abbe42c743808026477c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-7e28e08bfb7b1ae3208e2c91ea6f1e4e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-20ac675fffdeba0fbc242b392d3af8da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-849f8f0403e6b12fa8a9c24b84c094c7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f76666cf495bb0f0810313fe42e2c882&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3177468233ec37ad2a71dc38caa17377&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6adb64ff4e7b459fa1df815eac98b096&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-699c5fa3a4d8d093e6a599637df82443&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c14e1756e1d8d71ac3ba07125f122da&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e24261c10195e33e965bd7e966c33337&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-671e76af4a78aebc96c988f0504bfa99</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_27.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_148.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_57.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_40.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_74.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_165.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_67.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_65.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_15.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_150.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_121.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747658022</data>
    </node>
    <node id="advisory forum">
      <data key="d0">advisory forum</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">The advisory forum consists of stakeholders that provide advice and recommendations during the standardisation process for AI.&lt;SEP&gt;The advisory forum is established to provide technical expertise and advice to the Board and the Commission on AI-related tasks, representing a balanced selection of stakeholders from diverse sectors.&lt;SEP&gt;The advisory forum is mentioned as a consultative body that may provide input and oversight on the activities of the Board via invited representatives.&lt;SEP&gt;The advisory forum mentioned refers to a consultative body that the Commission consults when drafting common specifications and implementing acts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-010f171cc1a7f217689efe00fd8cb656&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2739984aae71aa27a0af867bc197b730&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-83ca16d466deaa751e3b9dc390e91e2f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ad86065dee426d0a1db7444552c4a385</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_40.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_67.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_41.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_65.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747658156</data>
    </node>
    <node id="notifying authorities">
      <data key="d0">notifying authorities</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Notifying authorities are entities that communicate relevant information about compliance and market practices to the Board and other bodies within this regulatory framework.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-83ca16d466deaa751e3b9dc390e91e2f</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_65.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Affected Person">
      <data key="d0">Affected Person</data>
      <data key="d1">person</data>
      <data key="d2">The affected person is an individual who experiences legal effects or significant impacts on their health, safety, or fundamental rights due to decisions made by a deployer based on AI system outputs.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-71f538bc77dea56a19150de3c7514990</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_86.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Union Law Exceptions">
      <data key="d0">Union Law Exceptions</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Union Law Exceptions encompass the specific legal provisions that may permit deviations or exceptions to the obligations placed on deployers of AI systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-71f538bc77dea56a19150de3c7514990</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_86.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Union AI Testing Support Structures">
      <data key="d0">Union AI Testing Support Structures</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Union AI testing support structures are designated organizations responsible for performing tasks and providing technical or scientific advice as per EU regulations on AI.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-d58805118916ede230a82a4ec3f60b3d</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_84.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Union market">
      <data key="d0">Union market</data>
      <data key="d1">geo</data>
      <data key="d2">The Union market refers to the collective market of the European Union where businesses operate and provide goods and services, including AI models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1ae06c8c76fc5976313d75e4ffabdfc</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_106.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="providers">
      <data key="d0">providers</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Providers are entities or businesses that place general-purpose AI models on the Union market, ensuring compliance with relevant regulations and obligations.&lt;SEP&gt;Providers refer to entities or organizations that offer general-purpose AI models and must comply with the established codes of practice.&lt;SEP&gt;Providers are entities that develop or distribute products containing AI systems and are responsible for ensuring regulatory compliance.&lt;SEP&gt;Providers refer to the entities involved in supplying high-risk AI systems and services, ensuring safe and reliable AI applications.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6de18717bcb0167956be85a15dd210f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-1952e0d35566561db978f8ee1ebdbd3b&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f1ae06c8c76fc5976313d75e4ffabdfc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8eae051373e28d701c4893438950556b</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_8.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_56.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_106.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_68.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747464086</data>
    </node>
    <node id="copyright and related rights">
      <data key="d0">copyright and related rights</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Copyright and related rights refer to legal protections established to safeguard the rights of creators and rightsholders over their works, relevant in the context of AI model training.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1ae06c8c76fc5976313d75e4ffabdfc</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_106.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Article 4(3) of Directive (EU) 2019/790">
      <data key="d0">Article 4(3) of Directive (EU) 2019/790</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Article 4(3) of Directive (EU) 2019/790 outlines specific obligations for providers concerning the reservation of rights by rightsholders.&lt;SEP&gt;This article outlines specific obligations regarding copyright compliance and the reservation of rights within the context of AI model training and usage in the EU.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f1ae06c8c76fc5976313d75e4ffabdfc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-212b902a4529db42951674775ac3ec73</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_106.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_104.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747464756</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Articles 53 and 55">
      <data key="d0">Articles 53 and 55</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Articles 53 and 55 refer to specific provisions within regulations that outline obligations related to the use of AI models and systemic risk management.&lt;SEP&gt;Articles 53 and 55 specify the obligations that must be adhered to by providers of general-purpose AI models within the Union.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6de18717bcb0167956be85a15dd210f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4dc0271c68856282748506ff58069b0</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_56.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Civil Society Organizations">
      <data key="d0">Civil Society Organizations</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Civil Society Organizations are non-governmental entities that can contribute to the drawing up of codes of practice and ensure diverse stakeholder involvement in AI regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;Civil society organizations play a crucial role in advocating for individuals' rights and participating in fundamental rights impact assessments.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-3a1d78baf29c07c65edc311f0ca04126&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4dc0271c68856282748506ff58069b0</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_96.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_56.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747657257</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Academia">
      <data key="d0">Academia</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Academia encompasses educational institutions and scholars contributing research and perspectives on the ethical implications of AI technologies.&lt;SEP&gt;Academia includes educational and research institutions that contribute expertise and knowledge in the formulation of codes of conduct for AI systems.&lt;SEP&gt;Academia refers to educational institutions and researchers that can provide insights and support in the development of AI-related codes of practice.&lt;SEP&gt;Academia involves educational institutions and scholars who bring research and theoretical insights regarding the Regulation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-40f3761e589fb7702938fd4fbbecfdcc&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f76666cf495bb0f0810313fe42e2c882&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-c4dc0271c68856282748506ff58069b0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c14e1756e1d8d71ac3ba07125f122da</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_56.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_165.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_150.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_95.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747657281</data>
    </node>
    <node id="codes of practice">
      <data key="d0">codes of practice</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Codes of practice are guidelines that providers of AI models can use to demonstrate compliance with legal obligations under AI regulations.&lt;SEP&gt;Codes of practice are specific guidelines established to ensure that the use of general-purpose AI models adheres to safety, ethical, and legal standards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-0c8a6b92fbe8d93eb878957c5ed60aa0&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6de18717bcb0167956be85a15dd210f</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_117.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_56.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747657432</data>
    </node>
    <node id="key performance indicators">
      <data key="d0">key performance indicators</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Key performance indicators are measurable values used to evaluate the success of the codes of practice in achieving their objectives.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-f6de18717bcb0167956be85a15dd210f</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_56.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="2 May 2025">
      <data key="d0">2 May 2025</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">2 May 2025 is the date by which codes of practice for AI providers must be established to ensure compliance with the regulation.&lt;SEP&gt;2 May 2025 is the deadline set for the finalization of the codes of practice related to AI usage within the Union.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-40a9e2a0fc4601f4b3d4f583384b6a06&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f6de18717bcb0167956be85a15dd210f</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_56.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_179.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747464818</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Article 55">
      <data key="d0">Article 55</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Article 55 includes additional obligations that providers must follow under the regulatory framework governing AI.&lt;SEP&gt;Article 55 may relate to compliance aspects within the same legal governance of AI models.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7c553a0db4b39033e7fcfeddeb912aac&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-daa2a9a43bd7a2482ad876b406b9e9c3</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_56.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_91.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747658139</data>
    </node>
    <node id="May 2025">
      <data key="d0">May 2025</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">May 2025 is the deadline by which codes of practice should be finalized according to the regulations set forth in the implementing acts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-daa2a9a43bd7a2482ad876b406b9e9c3</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_56.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="August 2025">
      <data key="d0">August 2025</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">August 2025 is the date by which finalization of code of practice must occur, or the Commission will intervene with implementing acts.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-daa2a9a43bd7a2482ad876b406b9e9c3</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_56.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Implementing Acts">
      <data key="d0">Implementing Acts</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Implementing Acts refer to specific actions taken to execute the provisions outlined in a legal framework, which may include various procedural guidelines.&lt;SEP&gt;Implementing acts are specific rules adopted by the European Commission to ensure uniform application of EU legislation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bec338e77f01240f23cac5480d2c8ae4&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d7805654ce0fe0d8d9b6ba008562d2aa</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_108.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_56.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747464292</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Examination Procedure">
      <data key="d0">Examination Procedure</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Examination Procedure represents a systematic method for evaluating and approving implementing acts, ensuring compliance with legislative standards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bec338e77f01240f23cac5480d2c8ae4</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_56.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="paragraph 2">
      <data key="d0">paragraph 2</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-bec338e77f01240f23cac5480d2c8ae4</data>
      <data key="d2">Article 98 includes issues set out in paragraph 2, indicating a direct connection between the concepts discussed in the article and the contents of that paragraph.</data>
      <data key="d1">UNKNOWN</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_56.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Regulation of the European Parliament and the Council">
      <data key="d0">Regulation of the European Parliament and the Council</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A regulation that outlines essential cybersecurity requirements for products with digital elements and high-risk AI systems.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60a12c037b4dee54159c11f348ff3f11</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_78.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="ENISA">
      <data key="d0">ENISA</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">ENISA is the European Union Agency for Cybersecurity responsible for providing cybersecurity solutions and frameworks for member states and organizations within the EU.&lt;SEP&gt;ENISA, the European Union Agency for Cybersecurity, is responsible for enhancing the cybersecurity framework within the EU and assists in issues related to AI systems.&lt;SEP&gt;ENISA, the European Union Agency for Cybersecurity, is a permanent member of the advisory forum, providing insights on cybersecurity issues.&lt;SEP&gt;ENISA is the European Union Agency for Cybersecurity that provides expertise and advice on cybersecurity aspects pertaining to the Regulation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ad86065dee426d0a1db7444552c4a385&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-f76666cf495bb0f0810313fe42e2c882&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-9ec0d2d3cbe2a53a3bbf6373c775edf2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60a12c037b4dee54159c11f348ff3f11</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_67.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_78.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_150.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747657281</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Regulation (EU) 2019/881">
      <data key="d0">Regulation (EU) 2019/881</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Regulation (EU) 2019/881 is a legislative act of the European Parliament and Council that outlines ENISA's tasks and cooperation on cybersecurity matters.&lt;SEP&gt;Regulation (EU) 2019/881 specifically focuses on cybersecurity measures and tasks for ENISA regarding the certification of digital products.&lt;SEP&gt;Regulation (EU) 2019/881 is a legislative measure concerning the governance of high-risk AI systems, focusing on data governance and cybersecurity requirements.&lt;SEP&gt;Regulation (EU) 2019/881 refers to the European Union regulation pertaining to cybersecurity for AI systems, including compliance and certification processes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ec0d2d3cbe2a53a3bbf6373c775edf2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60a12c037b4dee54159c11f348ff3f11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-07b79b1855840c81b5d9079a82c7e9fb&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4d423f15a7cbd742a57795f884434b60</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_42.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_122.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_78.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747657814</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Horizontal Cybersecurity Requirements">
      <data key="d0">Horizontal Cybersecurity Requirements</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A set of cybersecurity requirements that apply to various products with digital elements across the European Union.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60a12c037b4dee54159c11f348ff3f11</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_78.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Critical Products with Digital Elements">
      <data key="d0">Critical Products with Digital Elements</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Products that are deemed essential and require a high level of cybersecurity assurance under EU regulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-60a12c037b4dee54159c11f348ff3f11</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_78.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Conformity Assessment Procedure">
      <data key="d0">Conformity Assessment Procedure</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">The Conformity Assessment Procedure is a structured evaluation process defined in Annex VI, aimed at determining whether AI systems comply with established safety and rights standards. This procedure is crucial for ensuring that products, particularly high-risk AI systems, meet necessary regulatory and cybersecurity requirements before they are developed, deployed, and marketed. It encompasses a series of evaluations that providers must follow, which ensures that these systems adhere to legal standards and effectively protect users' rights and safety. Overall, the Conformity Assessment Procedure plays a vital role in validating that AI systems align with regulations designed to safeguard public interests.&lt;SEP&gt;The Conformity Assessment Procedure is a systematic process required for evaluating compliance of high-risk AI systems with established regulations and safety standards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7859a0c655f74761757b416c3b50bf36&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0c4d62babb1c5a144faeae6ac2f13628&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-a57bbcc3ccf00f26352f12c36036aedf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-60a12c037b4dee54159c11f348ff3f11&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-68d6983d829937a84d37118dacc3e39c&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5ab1f3b6cbb052c58ff3b9fc75a6d7a2&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-6c72b31b17ef0accd3df4ab25a063163</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_139.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_125.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_43.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_78.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_16.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_50.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747658111</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Regulation (EU) No 526/2013">
      <data key="d0">Regulation (EU) No 526/2013</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Regulation (EU) No 526/2013, known as the Cybersecurity Act, is the previous framework governing ENISA's operations before its revision under Regulation (EU) 2019/881.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ec0d2d3cbe2a53a3bbf6373c775edf2</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_78.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cybersecurity Act">
      <data key="d0">Cybersecurity Act</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">The Cybersecurity Act refers to Regulation (EU) No 526/2013 which was repealed and replaced by Regulation (EU) 2019/881, influencing the legal framework for cybersecurity in the EU.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ec0d2d3cbe2a53a3bbf6373c775edf2</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_78.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Cybersecurity of AI Systems">
      <data key="d0">Cybersecurity of AI Systems</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Cybersecurity of AI Systems refers to protecting artificial intelligence technologies from cyber threats and ensuring security standards are met.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-9ec0d2d3cbe2a53a3bbf6373c775edf2</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_78.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AI Innovation">
      <data key="d0">AI Innovation</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">AI Innovation refers to the development and application of new artificial intelligence technologies aimed at improving various sectors and ensuring compliance with legal frameworks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95424b9dff57302628c496570b4baff4</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_139.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Legal Framework">
      <data key="d0">Legal Framework</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A Legal Framework is a structured set of laws and regulations that govern the operation of technologies, particularly in ensuring ethical and safe development.&lt;SEP&gt;Legal Framework encompasses the set of regulations and standards that govern the development and implementation of AI systems, ensuring they meet compliance and ethical considerations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4cdf1c7e7ad7fe2c6b1b8f1b0b12db35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-95424b9dff57302628c496570b4baff4</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_139.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_138.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Evidence-Based Regulatory Learning">
      <data key="d0">Evidence-Based Regulatory Learning</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Evidence-Based Regulatory Learning is the process by which authorities gather insights from regulatory sandboxes to improve and adapt legal frameworks based on actual data and outcomes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95424b9dff57302628c496570b4baff4</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_139.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Emerging Risks">
      <data key="d0">Emerging Risks</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Emerging Risks refer to new potential dangers that arise from the development and deployment of AI technologies, which regulatory bodies aim to understand and mitigate.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95424b9dff57302628c496570b4baff4</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_139.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="National or European Standardisation Organisations">
      <data key="d0">National or European Standardisation Organisations</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">National or European Standardisation Organisations are entities that set technical standards for AI systems to ensure quality and interoperability within regulatory frameworks.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-95424b9dff57302628c496570b4baff4</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_139.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Stakeholder and Civil Society Organisations">
      <data key="d0">Stakeholder and Civil Society Organisations</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Stakeholder and civil society organisations involve various groups that engage in discussing and influencing AI policies and regulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7859a0c655f74761757b416c3b50bf36</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_139.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Standardisation Organisations">
      <data key="d0">Standardisation Organisations</data>
      <data key="d1">organization</data>
      <data key="d2">Standardisation Organisations are groups that develop and promote standards to ensure the quality and safety of products and services, including AI technologies.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-7859a0c655f74761757b416c3b50bf36</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_139.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AI Applications">
      <data key="d0">AI Applications</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">AI applications refer to various innovative technologies and software solutions that utilize artificial intelligence for different purposes.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-94b20fb25052b5f99e867bd24213f8f8</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/recital_139.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Regulation (EU) No 1025/2012">
      <data key="d0">Regulation (EU) No 1025/2012</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Regulation (EU) No 1025/2012 establishes a comprehensive framework for standardization procedures within the European Union, aiming to enhance both the consistency and quality of standards across member states. This regulation significantly influences the assessment of compliance for AI systems, as well as the broader landscape of national measures related to standardization. By promoting harmonized standards, Regulation (EU) No 1025/2012 fosters competitiveness and growth in the single market. It outlines the specific needs for clear and consistent standards, thus impacting the development and conformity processes not only for various industries but particularly for artificial intelligence systems within the EU.&lt;SEP&gt;Regulation (EU) No 1025/2012 outlines the procedures for standardisation and the role of the European Commission in assessing harmonised standards.&lt;SEP&gt;Regulation (EU) No 1025/2012 pertains to the European standardization policy, outlining procedures for the preparation and adoption of technical standards to facilitate trade.&lt;SEP&gt;Regulation (EU) No 1025/2012 pertains to the governance of standardization processes within the European Union.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-010f171cc1a7f217689efe00fd8cb656&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-0695d12d62d4fd15a528842231db1850&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-3177468233ec37ad2a71dc38caa17377&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-43e82a7af456b2ae813a7217f92c2008&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-8cc9a7191827dda78eda5924876e5f7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d6fc829d58f1a7bbf3defd5cbe758b8f&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee6d94a5f64203f09d02387acbdb39c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4f41337092de45470ab838aff17309ff&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2739984aae71aa27a0af867bc197b730&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-e24261c10195e33e965bd7e966c33337</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_40.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_41.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_3.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_81.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_121.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747658156</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AI Practices">
      <data key="d0">AI Practices</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">AI Practices encompass guidelines and prohibitions established to ensure the ethical and safe deployment of artificial intelligence technologies.&lt;SEP&gt;AI Practices encompass the set of regulations and ethical standards that must be followed for AI systems within the EU.&lt;SEP&gt;AI practices refer to the specific uses and methodologies of artificial intelligence systems that must comply with the regulations filed under the European Data Protection Regulation.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee6d94a5f64203f09d02387acbdb39c5&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-345a8ba89ed0ea3325462384d5e16aaf&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-5da2f6b1c2fa5619efa26719534e9c35</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_81.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_100.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/article_79.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747657782</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Article 79(5)">
      <data key="d0">Article 79(5)</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Article 79(5) outlines the notification requirements and timelines for responses by market surveillance authorities regarding AI regulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-ee6d94a5f64203f09d02387acbdb39c5</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_81.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Articles 40 and 41">
      <data key="d0">Articles 40 and 41</data>
      <data key="d1">event</data>
      <data key="d2">Articles 40 and 41 refer to specific sections within the EU regulation that detail harmonised standards and common specifications.&lt;SEP&gt;Articles 40 and 41 specify harmonised standards and common specifications which are critical for assessing AI compliance and practices.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8cc9a7191827dda78eda5924876e5f7e&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-ee6d94a5f64203f09d02387acbdb39c5</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_81.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="National Measure">
      <data key="d0">National Measure</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">A National Measure refers to any regulatory decision or action taken by a Member State that must comply with European Union regulations.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8cc9a7191827dda78eda5924876e5f7e</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_81.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Shortcomings in AI System">
      <data key="d0">Shortcomings in AI System</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Shortcomings in AI Systems refer to deficiencies that may lead to non-compliance with regulations, impacting their acceptance by the Commission.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-8cc9a7191827dda78eda5924876e5f7e</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_81.json</data>
    </node>
    <node id="AI Technologies">
      <data key="d0">AI Technologies</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">AI Technologies encompass a variety of tools and systems designed to enhance processes across various industries by leveraging data, algorithms, and automation.&lt;SEP&gt;AI Technologies refer to the suite of advanced systems and methods developed within the field of Artificial Intelligence, which require oversight for responsible innovation.&lt;SEP&gt;AI Technologies refer to the range of technologies related to artificial intelligence that national authorities must understand and regulate.&lt;SEP&gt;AI technologies encompass various advancements in artificial intelligence that require regular oversight and regulation for safety and compliance.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-22275ba6343521309923fef233affa2a&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-4cdf1c7e7ad7fe2c6b1b8f1b0b12db35&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-d42aaf56c15be020feabced14d2409d7&lt;SEP&gt;chunk-2a975f3ad5ea339c199a21dd569a7acb</data>
      <data key="d4">./cached_docs/article_70.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_138.json&lt;SEP&gt;./cached_docs/recital_4.json</data>
      <data key="d5">1747464838</data>
    </node>
    <node id="Regulatory Oversight">
      <data key="d0">Regulatory Oversight</data>
      <data key="d1">category</data>
      <data key="d2">Regulatory Oversight is the process of monitoring and controlling the development and deployment of technologies, ensuring they comply with legal frameworks and safety standards.</data>
      <data key="d3">chunk-4cdf1c7e7ad7fe2c6b1b8f1b0b12db35</data>
