    <edge source="Commission" target="Member States">
      <data key="d6">256.0</data>
      <data key="d7">The Commission and Member States jointly establish facilities that aid in the implementation of the Regulation.&lt;SEP&gt;The Commission oversees and ensures cooperation among Member States concerning the evaluation and compliance of AI systems.&lt;SEP&gt;The relationship between the Commission and the Member States is vital for the oversight and regulation of artificial intelligence (AI) systems within the European Union (EU). Member States communicate with the Commission regarding conformity assessments and the status of notified bodies, ensuring that all AI systems comply with established safety and compliance standards. They are required to submit reports to the Commission related to any changes in the designation of AI systems and inform the Commission about their compliance measures, including evaluations of non-compliance and any penalties enforced at the national level. 

The Commission supervises the regulatory framework that mandates compliance from Member States, promoting collaboration to ensure adherence to EU standards. This partnership is marked by regular and systematic exchanges of information, where Member States provide essential insights into compliance measures and are encouraged to communicate the identity and tasks of their national competent authorities. 

In their efforts to maintain regulatory consistency, the Commission conducts consultations with Member States, especially when objections arise concerning compliance or the authorization of high-risk AI systems. Through these consultations, they discuss potential amendments to regulations and jointly develop voluntary codes of conduct aimed at enhancing AI literacy and governance. Furthermore, the Commission notifies Member States about conformity assessment bodies and their compliance activities, fostering a unified approach to AI regulation.

Overall, the interplay between the Commission and Member States is foundational in creating a robust and coherent regulatory framework for AI systems in the EU. This framework emphasizes mutual responsibilities, effective communication, and a shared commitment to safeguarding public interests while ensuring market compliance across various jurisdictions. Member States also have the capacity to request updates to guidelines from the Commission, reinforcing the collaborative nature of their relationship.</data>
